00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:39.440
Tabitha.

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:41.890
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:50.840
BONILLA: Good morning.

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:52.680
My name is Tabitha
Bonilla, and I'm

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:56.890
a senior majoring in
political science and biology.

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:59.980
It is a great honor for me
to stand before you today

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:02.650
at a breakfast commemorating
Dr. Martin Luther

00:23:02.650 --> 00:23:06.010
King, Jr, a man
whose legacy has laid

00:23:06.010 --> 00:23:09.760
the foundation for our
continued pursuit of equality

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:12.070
and diversity.

00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:15.700
In his work, Dr. King divided
the civil rights movement

00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:17.750
into two components.

00:23:17.750 --> 00:23:21.850
The first exposed the
ugliness of racism,

00:23:21.850 --> 00:23:27.080
segregation, disenfranchisement,
hate, and violence.

00:23:27.080 --> 00:23:29.050
These include
attitudes, judgments,

00:23:29.050 --> 00:23:34.240
and actions directly victimizing
a certain race or ethnicity.

00:23:34.240 --> 00:23:36.160
The second of Dr.
King's civil rights

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:39.910
initiatives addressed what
he termed as the limitations

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:43.150
to our achievements, those
institutions preventing

00:23:43.150 --> 00:23:48.520
economic, academic, and
professional equality.

00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:51.430
To say that discrimination
still exists

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:54.280
should not be a
surprise to anyone.

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:58.960
We can all cite instances
of overt discrimination.

00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:02.500
When, for example,
the 2007 MLK seminars

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:06.070
installation commemorating
Dr. King's life and legacy

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:09.070
was vandalized in Lobby 10.

00:24:09.070 --> 00:24:12.730
Times when we were called
names, followed around stores,

00:24:12.730 --> 00:24:16.450
or had our abilities judged
on the basis of our skin color

00:24:16.450 --> 00:24:19.220
or the sound of our names.

00:24:19.220 --> 00:24:22.700
Likewise, we are all aware
of the shockingly low numbers

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:27.990
of underrepresented minority
faculty and graduate students.

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:31.280
We have even heard of the
disappointingly high number

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:35.570
of African-American, Latino,
and Native American students who

00:24:35.570 --> 00:24:38.330
did not complete their
undergraduate or high school

00:24:38.330 --> 00:24:40.720
education.

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:43.730
In academic institutions
such as MIT,

00:24:43.730 --> 00:24:47.150
we tend to concentrate
on these statistics.

00:24:47.150 --> 00:24:51.590
But what about those less
obvious ones of discrimination?

00:24:51.590 --> 00:24:55.070
It is still acceptable to crack
jokes about another culture

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:56.840
to get a good laugh.

00:24:56.840 --> 00:24:59.810
It is fine to make
assumptions about people

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:03.260
until those assumptions
are made about us.

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:07.080
But why do we
ignore those issues?

00:25:07.080 --> 00:25:11.330
We say it is because these forms
of racism are less recognizable

00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:15.140
and often because we are unaware
that we are participating

00:25:15.140 --> 00:25:17.830
in a form of discrimination.

00:25:17.830 --> 00:25:21.110
Unfortunately, these
subtle forms of prejudice

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:25.100
are the ones that tend to be
more prevalent in our society.

00:25:25.100 --> 00:25:27.980
For me, the most
interesting comments

00:25:27.980 --> 00:25:31.490
have been those related to
my understanding of diversity

00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:35.420
and my inclusion within
the minority community.

00:25:35.420 --> 00:25:37.640
Oddly enough,
these comments have

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:41.810
been made by people of almost
every race and ethnicity,

00:25:41.810 --> 00:25:46.880
with many coming from fellow
underrepresented minorities.

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:48.770
My understanding
of diversity has

00:25:48.770 --> 00:25:52.910
been challenged because I grew
up in Montana, a small state

00:25:52.910 --> 00:25:56.450
with few minorities.

00:25:56.450 --> 00:26:00.560
I've been told that a lack of
experience with discrimination

00:26:00.560 --> 00:26:03.590
precludes me from really
understanding civil rights

00:26:03.590 --> 00:26:06.920
issues, that because
my ancestors came

00:26:06.920 --> 00:26:09.050
of their own free will
to the United States,

00:26:09.050 --> 00:26:11.720
and not in chains, that
I could not possibly

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:13.940
understand the issues.

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:16.280
I have been criticized
by some because while I

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:22.400
claim a Hispanic heritage, I
speak more German than Spanish.

00:26:22.400 --> 00:26:24.950
People making these remarks
have been assuming things

00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:29.000
based on what they think they
know about my background.

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:31.010
What they do not know
is that my family

00:26:31.010 --> 00:26:33.380
has experienced
discrimination since they

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:35.690
entered this country.

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:38.150
My mother's family came
to the United States

00:26:38.150 --> 00:26:40.940
as German immigrants
seeking asylum because

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:43.460
of their Jewish heritage.

00:26:43.460 --> 00:26:47.510
Her father was later imprisoned
and tortured during World War

00:26:47.510 --> 00:26:49.160
II.

00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:51.913
My father's parents were
brought here as children.

00:26:51.913 --> 00:26:53.330
Both of their
families were trying

00:26:53.330 --> 00:26:56.210
to escape poverty in Mexico.

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:59.930
They subsequently relocated
to Montana from Texas,

00:26:59.930 --> 00:27:02.990
trying to escape discrimination.

00:27:02.990 --> 00:27:05.690
Despite their past
experiences, neither side

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:09.040
of my grandparents, much
like the rest of society,

00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:12.170
supported my parents'
interracial marriage.

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:14.600
Fortunately for me,
my parents did not

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:17.660
let these experiences
color their judgments.

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:21.110
Instead, they learned to value
their differences in culture

00:27:21.110 --> 00:27:23.750
and have incorporated components
of each other's background

00:27:23.750 --> 00:27:26.010
in our everyday life.

00:27:26.010 --> 00:27:28.790
This is why my mother now
eats her eggs with tortillas,

00:27:28.790 --> 00:27:33.013
instead of toast, and my father
eats sauerkraut on meatballs.

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:39.430
I did not mention this
story to suggest that we all

00:27:39.430 --> 00:27:42.190
need to lose or
necessarily combine

00:27:42.190 --> 00:27:44.410
our cultural identities.

00:27:44.410 --> 00:27:49.030
Rather, I aim to show that the
excellence found in diversity

00:27:49.030 --> 00:27:52.180
is lost when we fail to
appreciate and accept

00:27:52.180 --> 00:27:54.620
each other's differences.

00:27:54.620 --> 00:27:57.950
Unconsciously, we can all take
part in something seemingly

00:27:57.950 --> 00:28:01.010
innocuous that causes
us to classify others

00:28:01.010 --> 00:28:04.430
on the basis of
race and ethnicity.

00:28:04.430 --> 00:28:08.060
When we do this, the
cohesion and communication

00:28:08.060 --> 00:28:10.610
between different
ethnic and racial groups

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:15.830
suffers, and the diversity
no longer means progress.

00:28:15.830 --> 00:28:18.350
In order to achieve
true diversity,

00:28:18.350 --> 00:28:20.150
we must do more
than bring together

00:28:20.150 --> 00:28:23.390
a group of people with
different skin tones.

00:28:23.390 --> 00:28:26.660
We must learn to genuinely
appreciate and respect

00:28:26.660 --> 00:28:30.050
different backgrounds,
cultures, and beliefs.

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:32.810
Only then can we champion
the cause and work

00:28:32.810 --> 00:28:36.350
to complete the entirety
of Dr. King's dream.

00:28:36.350 --> 00:28:40.610
As we leave here today, let us
remember our individual parts

00:28:40.610 --> 00:28:43.490
in promoting diversity,
and only then will

00:28:43.490 --> 00:28:47.030
we be able to experience the
excellence that comes with it.

00:28:47.030 --> 00:28:47.903
Thank you.

00:28:47.903 --> 00:28:50.861
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.500
PEARCE: Thank you
very much, Tabitha.

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:11.720
Thank you for sharing
your experiences

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:14.300
and for your very
heartfelt reflections.

00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:15.860
Very moving.

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:18.260
We will now hear from
Ms. Elizabeth Clay.

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:19.588
Elizabeth.

00:29:19.588 --> 00:29:22.008
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:29.308 --> 00:29:30.933
ELIZABETH CLAY: Good
morning, everyone.

00:29:30.933 --> 00:29:31.891
AUDIENCE: Good morning.

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:35.320
ELIZABETH CLAY: My
name is Elizabeth Clay,

00:29:35.320 --> 00:29:38.140
and it is my honor to be able
to speak to the MIT community

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:40.960
today, to celebrate the life
of Reverend Dr. Martin Luther

00:29:40.960 --> 00:29:46.700
King, Jr. We convene in another
moment of contrast in America,

00:29:46.700 --> 00:29:48.350
a year that I believe
would make Dr.

00:29:48.350 --> 00:29:50.990
King proud, proud
of the achievement

00:29:50.990 --> 00:29:54.830
and potential of
political leaders,

00:29:54.830 --> 00:29:58.280
such as presidential candidate
Barack Obama, Massachusetts

00:29:58.280 --> 00:30:00.740
Governor Deval
Patrick, and a more

00:30:00.740 --> 00:30:04.130
diverse political
leadership in Congress.

00:30:04.130 --> 00:30:05.750
However, I believe
that he would still

00:30:05.750 --> 00:30:10.370
be angry about the neglect
of New Orleans and its people

00:30:10.370 --> 00:30:13.040
and the world's unwillingness to
save the lives of our brothers

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:15.930
and sisters in Darfur.

00:30:15.930 --> 00:30:18.330
Here at MIT, we're
not just observers

00:30:18.330 --> 00:30:20.940
of the world around us,
but we have the opportunity

00:30:20.940 --> 00:30:25.590
to be change agents and opinion
leaders on these issues.

00:30:25.590 --> 00:30:29.130
And I hope that MIT extends
socially just community

00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:31.890
supporting work in
New Orleans and commit

00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:35.670
to divest from companies
working in the Sudan.

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:38.190
This morning, a morning
I'm very sad to say

00:30:38.190 --> 00:30:41.310
finds our own community
at MIT divided.

00:30:41.310 --> 00:30:45.980
I'll focus my thoughts on the
theme diversity and excellence.

00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:48.090
The term diversity
has come to be focused

00:30:48.090 --> 00:30:51.870
on the inclusion of groups, such
as women and racial minorities,

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:54.850
who, historically, have been
locked out from institutions

00:30:54.850 --> 00:30:57.260
and processes of power.

00:30:57.260 --> 00:30:59.380
Only when all of the
talent comes to the table

00:30:59.380 --> 00:31:01.580
can true excellence emerge.

00:31:01.580 --> 00:31:05.480
This is the importance of
diversity as fair play.

00:31:05.480 --> 00:31:09.170
Dr. King dreamed of a world
of equality and justice,

00:31:09.170 --> 00:31:11.450
where every individual
has the opportunity

00:31:11.450 --> 00:31:15.980
to participate in and contribute
to institutions, communities,

00:31:15.980 --> 00:31:20.170
and practices that he
or she is qualified for.

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:22.000
I do believe that
this is generally

00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:25.030
a shared value at
MIT, but considering

00:31:25.030 --> 00:31:27.910
our society's deep
history of exclusion,

00:31:27.910 --> 00:31:31.060
more is required to
enable diverse populations

00:31:31.060 --> 00:31:34.030
to produce excellent outcomes.

00:31:34.030 --> 00:31:35.860
As a master's student
in urban planning,

00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:38.513
I often think about
what makes a city great.

00:31:38.513 --> 00:31:40.930
And while tastes vary about
the type of environment people

00:31:40.930 --> 00:31:43.090
choose to live
in, certain cities

00:31:43.090 --> 00:31:46.890
are magnets for international
and local migrants.

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:50.520
New York, London,
Mumbai, Sao Paolo all

00:31:50.520 --> 00:31:53.130
draw some of the best and
brightest, the most creative

00:31:53.130 --> 00:31:54.970
and most clever.

00:31:54.970 --> 00:31:58.690
New citizens dare to make their
marks there, and in doing so,

00:31:58.690 --> 00:32:02.000
make those cities
wealthy and vibrant.

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:04.160
Their excellence
and their diversity

00:32:04.160 --> 00:32:06.300
exist in a virtuous cycle.

00:32:06.300 --> 00:32:08.720
The constant flow of new
people from around the world

00:32:08.720 --> 00:32:11.490
makes them strong and adaptable.

00:32:11.490 --> 00:32:15.440
But it is often those who
bring so much to the city,

00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:19.310
with their labor, their ideas,
their new small businesses, who

00:32:19.310 --> 00:32:23.060
struggle to afford the cities
that they help to build.

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:26.630
Scholars have discussed the role
of Mexican-American immigrants

00:32:26.630 --> 00:32:29.780
in bringing economic and social
vitality to America's Sun Belt

00:32:29.780 --> 00:32:31.010
cities.

00:32:31.010 --> 00:32:32.600
Yet many of these
families struggle

00:32:32.600 --> 00:32:35.210
with stagnant wages,
limited services,

00:32:35.210 --> 00:32:38.870
and social intolerance
in the very same places.

00:32:38.870 --> 00:32:41.630
Similar economic and
social stigmatization

00:32:41.630 --> 00:32:44.240
faces construction workers
who build the gleaming

00:32:44.240 --> 00:32:48.260
towers of Mumbai, yet go
home to illegal settlements

00:32:48.260 --> 00:32:51.830
with no water and no toilets.

00:32:51.830 --> 00:32:55.580
Those who struggle continuously
to make a more tolerant nation,

00:32:55.580 --> 00:32:58.040
a nation that lives up to
its promises of fairness

00:32:58.040 --> 00:33:02.260
and justice, are frequently
made to suffer as well.

00:33:02.260 --> 00:33:05.680
Everyday activists who
fight sexism and homophobia

00:33:05.680 --> 00:33:09.230
are punished for daring
against the status quo.

00:33:09.230 --> 00:33:11.870
The price Dr. King had to
pay to make America a better

00:33:11.870 --> 00:33:15.410
place for all of us was
a lifetime of threats

00:33:15.410 --> 00:33:17.990
on him and on his
loved ones, finally

00:33:17.990 --> 00:33:20.920
ending in his own assassination.

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:24.460
Even today, in the 21st century,
Dalit people, formerly known

00:33:24.460 --> 00:33:27.220
as untouchables in
India, are killed

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:30.680
for objecting to
slavery-like work conditions.

00:33:30.680 --> 00:33:32.750
Their efforts to make the
country of their birth

00:33:32.750 --> 00:33:35.990
live up to the promises
of the constitution

00:33:35.990 --> 00:33:38.760
are rewarded with murder.

00:33:38.760 --> 00:33:42.270
Interpersonal bigotry and
institutional racism, sexism,

00:33:42.270 --> 00:33:44.580
and classism are
threats to building

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:46.890
a truly thriving
society and exist

00:33:46.890 --> 00:33:51.020
at MIT and all of
America's universities.

00:33:51.020 --> 00:33:54.090
The responsibility we have
as privileged people--

00:33:54.090 --> 00:33:56.370
and we are all likely
privileged in some ways,

00:33:56.370 --> 00:33:58.940
even if we are
marginalized in others--

00:33:58.940 --> 00:34:01.620
whether it's homeowners or
voters, or current students,

00:34:01.620 --> 00:34:04.460
faculty, and staff in
a leading university,

00:34:04.460 --> 00:34:06.260
is to create an
enabling environment

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:08.150
for diversity to
flourish and for people

00:34:08.150 --> 00:34:11.560
to reach their potential
unencumbered by prejudice.

00:34:11.560 --> 00:34:14.139
Promoting affordable housing,
providing basic amenities

00:34:14.139 --> 00:34:16.540
to all residents allows
all of the city's workers

00:34:16.540 --> 00:34:19.820
to benefit from the city
that they have created.

00:34:19.820 --> 00:34:23.420
Expressing tolerance and
rejecting bigoted acts

00:34:23.420 --> 00:34:25.100
reduces the heavy
emotional costs

00:34:25.100 --> 00:34:27.409
on individuals who
have been marginalized

00:34:27.409 --> 00:34:31.440
and lift burdens that threaten
their chances for success.

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:33.980
And here at MIT,
while we all excel

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:36.810
in some part of our
careers or our studies,

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:38.750
we have not yet cracked
the code on building

00:34:38.750 --> 00:34:41.770
a fully tolerant and
supportive society.

00:34:41.770 --> 00:34:44.380
This divisive moment,
with allegations

00:34:44.380 --> 00:34:47.230
of racism swirling
through the halls,

00:34:47.230 --> 00:34:51.130
is an opportunity to
address a forbidden topic.

00:34:51.130 --> 00:34:55.070
The Institute's
leadership must do more.

00:34:55.070 --> 00:34:58.670
We must seize this moment
to do more than just talk.

00:34:58.670 --> 00:35:01.580
They can take this
opportunity to listen.

00:35:01.580 --> 00:35:05.580
Listen to the concerns and fears
and hopes of students, faculty,

00:35:05.580 --> 00:35:07.110
and staff.

00:35:07.110 --> 00:35:10.820
And then to act together to
create a more tolerant and open

00:35:10.820 --> 00:35:13.640
community, where people
of all backgrounds feel

00:35:13.640 --> 00:35:16.890
valued for their contributions.

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:19.320
The GSE Diversity Committee
has made an attempt

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:20.910
to begin these
type of discussions

00:35:20.910 --> 00:35:22.740
among graduate students.

00:35:22.740 --> 00:35:25.530
But sadly, it faces
resistance from some students

00:35:25.530 --> 00:35:27.090
who think that
diversity is a passing

00:35:27.090 --> 00:35:30.090
trend or an attempt
for minorities

00:35:30.090 --> 00:35:32.690
to receive special treatment.

00:35:32.690 --> 00:35:34.760
The graduate body
at MIT continues

00:35:34.760 --> 00:35:37.730
to have shamefully low numbers
of underrepresented minority

00:35:37.730 --> 00:35:39.490
graduate students.

00:35:39.490 --> 00:35:42.810
And at a campus discussion
last week on cultural identity,

00:35:42.810 --> 00:35:45.860
many international students said
that they feel voiceless here

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:49.460
at MIT and feel emotionally
exhausted by being

00:35:49.460 --> 00:35:52.210
misunderstood by colleagues.

00:35:52.210 --> 00:35:54.460
We are all reduced
intellectually,

00:35:54.460 --> 00:35:58.440
spiritually, and morally
when members of our community

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:01.530
are silenced or
silence themselves out

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:04.050
of fear and discomfort.

00:36:04.050 --> 00:36:06.820
For the GSE to vote for a
permanent diversity committee

00:36:06.820 --> 00:36:10.780
at next month's meeting would
be a step in the right direction

00:36:10.780 --> 00:36:13.780
for the student body to say
that a tolerant environment is

00:36:13.780 --> 00:36:15.910
important, both for
current students

00:36:15.910 --> 00:36:20.480
and to attract the best
talent from around the world.

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:23.060
Fear of change
paralyzes the privileged

00:36:23.060 --> 00:36:27.380
as vigorously as hope for
change rouses the marginalized.

00:36:27.380 --> 00:36:29.810
Therefore, leaving the challenge
of creating a supportive

00:36:29.810 --> 00:36:33.920
atmosphere for excellence to
emerge to individuals alone

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:35.360
is untenable.

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:37.490
Coalitions of people
and institutions

00:36:37.490 --> 00:36:39.500
should work
proactively to create

00:36:39.500 --> 00:36:42.830
a more positive and enabling
environment for diversity

00:36:42.830 --> 00:36:45.330
and excellence to converge.

00:36:45.330 --> 00:36:48.590
This is not a favor from
rich to poor or from one race

00:36:48.590 --> 00:36:51.980
to another, but an investment
in a more fair and prosperous

00:36:51.980 --> 00:36:53.330
world.

00:36:53.330 --> 00:36:56.300
Again, we at MIT are not
just observers of the world

00:36:56.300 --> 00:36:59.900
around us, but have the
opportunity and responsibility

00:36:59.900 --> 00:37:01.219
to make change.

00:37:01.219 --> 00:37:02.686
Thank you.

00:37:02.686 --> 00:37:05.620
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:22.260 --> 00:37:24.290
PEARCE: Thank you
very much, Elizabeth.

00:37:24.290 --> 00:37:26.720
And certainly, we
accept your challenge

00:37:26.720 --> 00:37:29.810
to be positive agents
of change in our world.

00:37:29.810 --> 00:37:32.450
Once again, I'd like to thank
both Tabitha and Elizabeth

00:37:32.450 --> 00:37:35.710
for their very poignant
and timely words

00:37:35.710 --> 00:37:37.490
and for letting the
spirit of Dr. King

00:37:37.490 --> 00:37:40.397
be renewed in this
contemporary context.

00:37:40.397 --> 00:37:43.259
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:52.840
Now I have the honor of inviting
Chancellor Phillip Clay to come

00:37:52.840 --> 00:37:58.000
and recognize the 2006, 2007
Martin Luther King Leadership

00:37:58.000 --> 00:37:59.120
awardees.

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:00.720
Chancellor Clay.

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:03.660
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:16.420 --> 00:38:18.520
PHILLIP CLAY: I want to
thank both of our student

00:38:18.520 --> 00:38:24.310
speakers for their great remarks
and for their challenges to us.

00:38:24.310 --> 00:38:29.790
Dr. King was first and
foremost a preacher.

00:38:29.790 --> 00:38:32.700
And one of the lessons,
though, from a sermon

00:38:32.700 --> 00:38:36.540
that is a favorite
of mine has to do

00:38:36.540 --> 00:38:39.470
with the meaning of leadership.

00:38:39.470 --> 00:38:43.700
And in a discussion with
a couple of his disciples,

00:38:43.700 --> 00:38:48.090
one of whom asked to
be awarded leadership,

00:38:48.090 --> 00:38:52.160
he indicated to them
that leadership is not

00:38:52.160 --> 00:38:55.960
awarded, that leadership
is given to those

00:38:55.960 --> 00:38:59.080
who are first servants--

00:38:59.080 --> 00:39:04.600
those who serve and serve
in an important way.

00:39:04.600 --> 00:39:09.440
They are awarded
leadership by their peers.

00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:13.060
We had a wonderful
dinner last night

00:39:13.060 --> 00:39:16.480
to celebrate the awardees,
and we won't go over

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:20.800
the whole program, but I do
want to introduce the awardees

00:39:20.800 --> 00:39:24.040
and give you a little
bit of a sense of why

00:39:24.040 --> 00:39:28.630
these servants have been
awarded the leadership

00:39:28.630 --> 00:39:31.750
awards from last night.

00:39:31.750 --> 00:39:36.522
First of all, let me ask Mr.
Nathaniel Gerald to stand.

00:39:36.522 --> 00:39:39.414
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:47.620 --> 00:39:51.010
Nathaniel has been
at MIT for many years

00:39:51.010 --> 00:39:54.580
and has worked in the mail room,
but his most important service

00:39:54.580 --> 00:39:59.710
to MIT has been in his
role as community glue.

00:39:59.710 --> 00:40:05.980
Now many of us, especially
those of you who are new to MIT

00:40:05.980 --> 00:40:09.250
and who believe all of
your news comes from email,

00:40:09.250 --> 00:40:12.670
there are those of us who
remember and appreciate

00:40:12.670 --> 00:40:15.460
the great role that Gerald
and others of his colleagues

00:40:15.460 --> 00:40:19.900
played in coming to
visit us each morning

00:40:19.900 --> 00:40:23.320
to bring us information
material, good news

00:40:23.320 --> 00:40:26.500
and bad news from other
parts of the Institute.

00:40:26.500 --> 00:40:30.070
So his role in
providing this service

00:40:30.070 --> 00:40:32.590
over many years,
something many of us

00:40:32.590 --> 00:40:37.030
remember with great fondness,
represents an important way

00:40:37.030 --> 00:40:39.920
that he has served the MIT
community for many years.

00:40:39.920 --> 00:40:41.293
Thank you very much, Gerald.

00:40:41.293 --> 00:40:43.758
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:52.140 --> 00:40:56.166
Let me ask Michelle Harton
and Austin Harton to stand.

00:40:56.166 --> 00:40:59.082
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:08.760
These two alumni serve
us in an important way.

00:41:08.760 --> 00:41:12.000
Not only have they had
outstanding professional

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:16.800
careers in Chicago and have
served in a variety of roles

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:23.100
at the Chicago chapter
of Black Alumni of MIT,

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:30.300
they have set a model for how
to do the kind of outreach which

00:41:30.300 --> 00:41:33.900
goes a long way to make
MIT a great institution

00:41:33.900 --> 00:41:37.200
and to make sure this continues.

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:41.820
They and their colleagues were
responsible for recruiting

00:41:41.820 --> 00:41:50.550
seven of the class of 2007
black students at MIT.

00:41:50.550 --> 00:41:55.350
Their role in outreach to dozens
of schools in the Chicago area

00:41:55.350 --> 00:41:58.620
made our task especially easy.

00:41:58.620 --> 00:42:01.860
Not only did they provide
the kind of outreach

00:42:01.860 --> 00:42:04.380
to these seven students,
but they provided service

00:42:04.380 --> 00:42:08.340
to many dozens of students,
who, whether they come to MIT

00:42:08.340 --> 00:42:12.870
or not, will be part of the
advancement in STEM fields

00:42:12.870 --> 00:42:15.130
that will be so important to us.

00:42:15.130 --> 00:42:18.510
So we honor their
initiative and service

00:42:18.510 --> 00:42:21.780
in leading us to these
outstanding young men

00:42:21.780 --> 00:42:22.497
and women.

00:42:22.497 --> 00:42:23.330
Thank you very much.

00:42:23.330 --> 00:42:26.216
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:31.040 --> 00:42:32.896
La Tonya Green.

00:42:32.896 --> 00:42:35.794
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:42.560 --> 00:42:46.180
La Tonya is a PhD student in
urban studies and planning,

00:42:46.180 --> 00:42:50.060
but we honor her for a
couple of other roles.

00:42:50.060 --> 00:42:53.790
First of all, upon completing
her master's degree,

00:42:53.790 --> 00:42:58.610
she went to work for about
three years in the Paterson, New

00:42:58.610 --> 00:43:03.560
Jersey schools and developed
an outstanding program

00:43:03.560 --> 00:43:07.530
that linked public education,
secondary education,

00:43:07.530 --> 00:43:11.180
to important aspects of
community development,

00:43:11.180 --> 00:43:16.160
using the skill and
methods of design

00:43:16.160 --> 00:43:20.480
to provide an extra
leavening to high school

00:43:20.480 --> 00:43:23.630
reform in Paterson, New Jersey.

00:43:23.630 --> 00:43:27.140
I also want to
remind our colleagues

00:43:27.140 --> 00:43:30.440
that she has played an important
role as a student leader

00:43:30.440 --> 00:43:34.010
both in the first incarnation
of her time at MIT

00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:37.920
as a graduate student and more
recently in her time as a PhD

00:43:37.920 --> 00:43:38.540
student.

00:43:38.540 --> 00:43:39.783
Thank you very much.

00:43:39.783 --> 00:43:42.248
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:47.190 --> 00:43:51.349
Finally, I will ask
Ike Colbert to stand.

00:43:51.349 --> 00:43:53.844
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:35.050 --> 00:44:36.970
Is there anybody
here who doesn't

00:44:36.970 --> 00:44:38.085
know that Ike is retiring?

00:44:42.470 --> 00:44:45.500
I warned Ike a couple of years
ago about the long goodbye.

00:44:48.170 --> 00:44:49.910
And I advised him
earlier this week

00:44:49.910 --> 00:44:55.640
that he should begin to prepare
some remarks for these events.

00:44:55.640 --> 00:45:01.250
I need not say much
more, except that Ike,

00:45:01.250 --> 00:45:05.870
both in his role as an
administrative officer

00:45:05.870 --> 00:45:09.980
and for the last dozen
years in the dean's office

00:45:09.980 --> 00:45:13.370
and the last seven years as
dean of graduate students,

00:45:13.370 --> 00:45:17.540
Ike has played a very,
very special role

00:45:17.540 --> 00:45:22.130
to many of us who have had the
pleasure of being his colleague

00:45:22.130 --> 00:45:25.760
and to generations of
graduate students, some

00:45:25.760 --> 00:45:28.940
of whom-- all of whom benefited
greatly from it, though some

00:45:28.940 --> 00:45:33.350
of them had to visit him more
than once to get the message.

00:45:33.350 --> 00:45:36.740
He is nevertheless
a person who has

00:45:36.740 --> 00:45:40.250
served MIT with great
distinction and dedication.

00:45:40.250 --> 00:45:42.900
And for that service,
we are grateful.

00:45:42.900 --> 00:45:44.069
Thank you very much.

00:45:44.069 --> 00:45:47.003
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:59.230 --> 00:46:01.420
PEARCE: Thank you very
much, Chancellor Clay.

00:46:01.420 --> 00:46:03.250
On a personal note,
I must mention

00:46:03.250 --> 00:46:06.400
that I am particularly
pleased that the Hartons were

00:46:06.400 --> 00:46:08.600
recognized for their
tireless efforts.

00:46:08.600 --> 00:46:12.550
In fact, I must say that I am
one of the now famous Chicago

00:46:12.550 --> 00:46:14.434
Seven.

00:46:14.434 --> 00:46:16.874
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:21.280 --> 00:46:23.140
Congratulations to each of you.

00:46:23.140 --> 00:46:28.390
Your tireless efforts
have not gone unnoticed.

00:46:28.390 --> 00:46:32.290
The Black Students Union lounge
has had an impact on many of us

00:46:32.290 --> 00:46:34.090
in the MIT community.

00:46:34.090 --> 00:46:36.850
Here to reflect on the
impact of the lounge

00:46:36.850 --> 00:46:39.100
and to speak about the
recent improvements,

00:46:39.100 --> 00:46:41.110
please welcome Mr.
Richard Burgess,

00:46:41.110 --> 00:46:44.140
a sophomore in the Department
of Civil Engineering

00:46:44.140 --> 00:46:46.000
and Environmental Engineering.

00:46:46.000 --> 00:46:49.478
And he is also senior co-chair
of the Black Students Union.

00:46:49.478 --> 00:46:50.434
Richard.

00:46:50.434 --> 00:46:53.302
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:58.090 --> 00:47:00.460
BURGESS: Good morning.

00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:04.390
Next year will mark
the 40th anniversary

00:47:04.390 --> 00:47:08.410
of the pivotal events that
led to a seven-fold increase

00:47:08.410 --> 00:47:10.300
in the number of
students of color

00:47:10.300 --> 00:47:14.950
who entered MIT freshman
class over the previous year.

00:47:14.950 --> 00:47:17.560
At that time, the
Black Student Union

00:47:17.560 --> 00:47:21.070
was led by Dr. Shirley
Ann Jackson, now

00:47:21.070 --> 00:47:24.250
the president of RPI.

00:47:24.250 --> 00:47:28.180
She and her colleagues listed as
one of the 12 requests to then

00:47:28.180 --> 00:47:33.940
President Howard Johnson
the establishment of, quote,

00:47:33.940 --> 00:47:38.620
"an Afro-American center run
by the MIT Black Student Union,

00:47:38.620 --> 00:47:41.800
where students can meet and
where examples of black culture

00:47:41.800 --> 00:47:44.350
could be on display."

00:47:44.350 --> 00:47:48.400
This center, called
the BSU Lounge,

00:47:48.400 --> 00:47:51.790
is located outside
those doors and has

00:47:51.790 --> 00:47:55.010
undergone major renovations.

00:47:55.010 --> 00:47:58.730
This week, the BSU will be
celebrating the renovation

00:47:58.730 --> 00:48:02.630
of the Black Students Union
lounge, a safe student

00:48:02.630 --> 00:48:05.420
space that has been affiliated
with the organization

00:48:05.420 --> 00:48:08.020
since 1968.

00:48:08.020 --> 00:48:11.320
Over the past few years,
the members of the BSU

00:48:11.320 --> 00:48:14.430
have seen the need to
revitalize the lounge.

00:48:14.430 --> 00:48:17.610
And through the generosity
of the Chancellor's Office,

00:48:17.610 --> 00:48:22.160
the black alumni of MIT,
and student life programs,

00:48:22.160 --> 00:48:24.770
we have been able to make
significant enhancements

00:48:24.770 --> 00:48:27.060
to this space.

00:48:27.060 --> 00:48:30.840
These additions include
new audio visual system,

00:48:30.840 --> 00:48:34.460
new lighting, and
new furnishings.

00:48:34.460 --> 00:48:36.580
At the conclusion
of this program,

00:48:36.580 --> 00:48:38.660
I invite you to
view the lounge so

00:48:38.660 --> 00:48:43.170
that you may see for yourselves
the improvements to the space.

00:48:43.170 --> 00:48:45.670
Armed with this newly
renovated lounge,

00:48:45.670 --> 00:48:48.180
the Black Students
Union is fully equipped

00:48:48.180 --> 00:48:52.530
to realize its dual goal of
creating a sense of community

00:48:52.530 --> 00:48:57.390
for black students at MIT
and impacting both the MIT

00:48:57.390 --> 00:48:59.196
and greater Boston community.

00:48:59.196 --> 00:49:00.188
Thank you.

00:49:00.188 --> 00:49:02.668
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:13.110 --> 00:49:14.660
PEARCE: Thank you
very much, Ricky.

00:49:14.660 --> 00:49:17.645
As he mentioned and
I also encourage you,

00:49:17.645 --> 00:49:19.520
please visit the Black
Students Union lounge.

00:49:19.520 --> 00:49:21.650
It's right outside
these doors here.

00:49:21.650 --> 00:49:25.310
Please visit the lounge
later on this morning.

00:49:25.310 --> 00:49:29.060
It now gives me great pleasure
to welcome the 16th president

00:49:29.060 --> 00:49:32.270
of MIT, Dr. Susan Hockfield.

00:49:32.270 --> 00:49:34.790
She will give some remarks
and speak to MIT's congruence

00:49:34.790 --> 00:49:37.370
of diversity and excellence.

00:49:37.370 --> 00:49:41.000
After her remarks, she will
introduce our keynote speaker,

00:49:41.000 --> 00:49:42.190
Mr. Ted Childs.

00:49:42.190 --> 00:49:43.648
President Hockfield.

00:49:43.648 --> 00:49:46.564
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:56.128 --> 00:49:57.586
HOCKFIELD: Good
morning, everybody.

00:49:57.586 --> 00:49:59.330
AUDIENCE: Good morning.

00:49:59.330 --> 00:50:02.090
HOCKFIELD: I want to add
my thanks to everyone's--

00:50:02.090 --> 00:50:06.260
to the organizing committee for
this year's Martin Luther King,

00:50:06.260 --> 00:50:08.270
Jr. celebration.

00:50:08.270 --> 00:50:13.280
As was mentioned earlier, this
is the 33rd annual celebration

00:50:13.280 --> 00:50:16.070
of Dr. King's life and legacy.

00:50:16.070 --> 00:50:21.380
Extraordinary comment on MIT's
dedication to these principles.

00:50:21.380 --> 00:50:26.600
Special thanks go to Professor
Michael Feld and Dean Karl Reid

00:50:26.600 --> 00:50:28.970
for their work as co-chairs.

00:50:28.970 --> 00:50:31.970
I would mention that Professor
Feld's leadership in particular

00:50:31.970 --> 00:50:34.760
has been critical to the
success of this annual event

00:50:34.760 --> 00:50:37.070
for many years, and
I want to invite

00:50:37.070 --> 00:50:38.906
you to join me in thanking him.

00:50:38.906 --> 00:50:41.321
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:50.030 --> 00:50:52.220
I also want to add
my congratulations

00:50:52.220 --> 00:50:55.340
to those that have already been
spoken to this year's Martin

00:50:55.340 --> 00:50:58.520
Luther King, Jr. Award winners.

00:50:58.520 --> 00:51:04.430
Congratulations, Nathaniel, La
Tonya, Michelle, and Austin.

00:51:04.430 --> 00:51:08.610
And special thanks to you, Ike.

00:51:08.610 --> 00:51:12.180
I've known Dean Ike Colbert and
his commitment to the Institute

00:51:12.180 --> 00:51:16.650
and its students from my first
years in academic leadership,

00:51:16.650 --> 00:51:19.980
long before I arrived at MIT.

00:51:19.980 --> 00:51:21.930
I met Ike when I
had just started

00:51:21.930 --> 00:51:25.980
as dean of the graduate
school at Yale University,

00:51:25.980 --> 00:51:29.370
and he served as a very
powerful mentor to me

00:51:29.370 --> 00:51:30.583
in those early years.

00:51:30.583 --> 00:51:32.250
So thank you on behalf
of the Institute,

00:51:32.250 --> 00:51:34.098
and thank you personally.

00:51:34.098 --> 00:51:37.032
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:42.420 --> 00:51:45.540
And finally, and certainly
last is not least,

00:51:45.540 --> 00:51:47.600
I want to thank this
year's student speakers.

00:51:47.600 --> 00:51:51.830
Tabitha, Elizabeth, and Richard,
thank you so much, so much

00:51:51.830 --> 00:51:55.874
for inspiring us with
your powerful remarks.

00:51:55.874 --> 00:51:58.826
[APPLAUSE]

00:52:03.760 --> 00:52:05.740
Now I want to begin
my own remarks

00:52:05.740 --> 00:52:09.730
with a brief
personal reflection.

00:52:09.730 --> 00:52:12.040
And then I'm going to
let that short reflection

00:52:12.040 --> 00:52:17.160
lead into some thoughts
on where we are today

00:52:17.160 --> 00:52:18.535
and where we want
to be tomorrow.

00:52:21.330 --> 00:52:26.040
On January 15 this year
was the national day

00:52:26.040 --> 00:52:30.480
of celebration of the life
and legacy of Dr. King.

00:52:30.480 --> 00:52:33.930
Part of my own personal
reflection for the day

00:52:33.930 --> 00:52:37.920
was to reread the words
of Dr. King in his now

00:52:37.920 --> 00:52:42.020
iconic "I Have a Dream" speech.

00:52:42.020 --> 00:52:44.960
The speech never
fails to move me

00:52:44.960 --> 00:52:49.150
as it did when I first heard
it now many, many years ago

00:52:49.150 --> 00:52:52.820
and as it has moved and
inspired millions of others

00:52:52.820 --> 00:52:55.880
in the nearly 45
years since it was

00:52:55.880 --> 00:53:00.140
delivered on the steps
of the Lincoln Memorial.

00:53:00.140 --> 00:53:04.040
Every time I read that
speech, some passages

00:53:04.040 --> 00:53:08.180
seemed to me a well-known
well-loved tune.

00:53:08.180 --> 00:53:11.960
And every time I read
it, at least one passage

00:53:11.960 --> 00:53:16.470
sings a new song to me and
strikes me in a new way.

00:53:16.470 --> 00:53:19.490
So it is with only the
most powerful expressions

00:53:19.490 --> 00:53:22.420
of the human spirit.

00:53:22.420 --> 00:53:26.230
This year, one passage
in particular really

00:53:26.230 --> 00:53:28.660
just jumped off the page.

00:53:28.660 --> 00:53:30.790
It harmonized with
my own reflections,

00:53:30.790 --> 00:53:33.910
and it joined with my
thoughts on our work

00:53:33.910 --> 00:53:37.310
together here at MIT.

00:53:37.310 --> 00:53:41.110
As Dr. King describes our
forward path toward justice

00:53:41.110 --> 00:53:48.190
for all, he says we
cannot walk alone.

00:53:48.190 --> 00:53:52.540
And as we walk, we
must make the pledge

00:53:52.540 --> 00:53:55.840
that we shall
always march ahead.

00:53:55.840 --> 00:54:00.010
We cannot turn back.

00:54:00.010 --> 00:54:02.950
I believe that the
most powerful way

00:54:02.950 --> 00:54:06.100
we can honor Dr. King
at this annual breakfast

00:54:06.100 --> 00:54:09.610
is to assess our
progress on the march

00:54:09.610 --> 00:54:13.360
and to affirm our commitment
as a community to accelerate

00:54:13.360 --> 00:54:14.530
the pace of change.

00:54:17.200 --> 00:54:20.620
We're gathered this morning at
a painful moment for the MIT

00:54:20.620 --> 00:54:23.110
community.

00:54:23.110 --> 00:54:25.000
Professor James
Shirley has raised

00:54:25.000 --> 00:54:28.900
issues that reach beyond
any single individual

00:54:28.900 --> 00:54:32.140
or any single institution.

00:54:32.140 --> 00:54:35.560
In this difficult time,
our deepest concern,

00:54:35.560 --> 00:54:40.690
our deepest concern is for
Professor Shirley's health

00:54:40.690 --> 00:54:45.040
and for the impact of this
situation on his family--

00:54:45.040 --> 00:54:48.280
his wife Marian, who is a
respected and beloved member

00:54:48.280 --> 00:54:53.590
of our community, and for
their two young daughters.

00:54:53.590 --> 00:54:58.300
But our concern also reaches
into the larger MIT community

00:54:58.300 --> 00:55:04.870
because, as Dr. King reminds
us, none of us walks alone.

00:55:04.870 --> 00:55:07.465
We will only move ahead
if we do so together.

00:55:10.060 --> 00:55:15.830
If MIT today is to advance its
historic mission of teaching,

00:55:15.830 --> 00:55:21.160
research, and service, we simply
must increase the opportunities

00:55:21.160 --> 00:55:25.990
for minority faculty,
students, and staff.

00:55:25.990 --> 00:55:31.000
Let me first speak
about our faculty.

00:55:31.000 --> 00:55:34.420
Our faculty itself has made
an important commitment

00:55:34.420 --> 00:55:38.380
to accelerating our progress
in the May 2004 faculty

00:55:38.380 --> 00:55:40.150
resolution.

00:55:40.150 --> 00:55:43.750
Overall, the number of minority
faculty at the Institute

00:55:43.750 --> 00:55:45.370
has doubled--

00:55:45.370 --> 00:55:48.070
doubled since 1990.

00:55:48.070 --> 00:55:53.140
However, the numbers
are still way too small.

00:55:53.140 --> 00:55:57.910
As of October 2005, less
than 5% of our faculty

00:55:57.910 --> 00:56:01.390
were members of minority groups.

00:56:01.390 --> 00:56:05.260
I say that, but I follow
quickly with reminding us

00:56:05.260 --> 00:56:09.580
that there are some
encouraging signs of progress.

00:56:09.580 --> 00:56:15.310
First, of the new faculty
who arrived at MIT this year,

00:56:15.310 --> 00:56:18.910
11 and 1/2% are members of
underrepresented minority

00:56:18.910 --> 00:56:20.350
groups.

00:56:20.350 --> 00:56:21.910
You heard me correctly--

00:56:21.910 --> 00:56:25.660
11 and 1/2% this year.

00:56:25.660 --> 00:56:29.470
This year, we welcomed to
campus eight Martin Luther

00:56:29.470 --> 00:56:33.130
King, Jr. visiting professors
and scholars, more than ever

00:56:33.130 --> 00:56:36.190
before in this program.

00:56:36.190 --> 00:56:38.050
And we together
have been working

00:56:38.050 --> 00:56:40.030
toward an Institute
leadership that reflects

00:56:40.030 --> 00:56:42.180
the diversity of our community.

00:56:42.180 --> 00:56:45.370
In our departments, our
labs, our centers, and at

00:56:45.370 --> 00:56:47.740
the level of the
Academic Council,

00:56:47.740 --> 00:56:51.820
our academic and administrative
leadership is now more diverse

00:56:51.820 --> 00:56:54.880
than it has ever been before.

00:56:54.880 --> 00:57:00.770
Still, we remain acutely aware
that the numbers remain small,

00:57:00.770 --> 00:57:03.080
much smaller than we'd like.

00:57:03.080 --> 00:57:06.010
But as we chart the course
of our walk together,

00:57:06.010 --> 00:57:10.060
we can point to powerful proof
that concerted institutional

00:57:10.060 --> 00:57:13.330
effort can make a difference.

00:57:13.330 --> 00:57:19.720
That proof lies in MIT's own
ongoing work in gender equity.

00:57:19.720 --> 00:57:22.240
The study of women faculty
in the School of Science

00:57:22.240 --> 00:57:24.280
and its successors
in the other schools

00:57:24.280 --> 00:57:29.260
had a powerful impact not just
at MIT, but around the nation

00:57:29.260 --> 00:57:31.330
and around the world.

00:57:31.330 --> 00:57:34.360
We want our new initiative
on minority faculty issues

00:57:34.360 --> 00:57:36.460
recently announced
by the provost

00:57:36.460 --> 00:57:38.800
to have the same
catalytic impact

00:57:38.800 --> 00:57:42.160
and to demonstrate the same kind
of institutional and national

00:57:42.160 --> 00:57:43.990
leadership.

00:57:43.990 --> 00:57:46.960
This new initiative will
build on important work

00:57:46.960 --> 00:57:49.060
over the past year.

00:57:49.060 --> 00:57:52.540
The review of the Martin Luther
King, Jr. Scholars Program,

00:57:52.540 --> 00:57:55.960
charged by Provost
Reif a year ago,

00:57:55.960 --> 00:57:57.910
has produced some
important new ideas

00:57:57.910 --> 00:58:00.250
about how this program
might help accelerate

00:58:00.250 --> 00:58:02.560
our progress in
bringing new minority

00:58:02.560 --> 00:58:05.180
scholars to our campus.

00:58:05.180 --> 00:58:07.570
And we've announced
plans for a new office

00:58:07.570 --> 00:58:10.450
of the Associate Provost
for Faculty Equity,

00:58:10.450 --> 00:58:15.130
and we're now in an active
search for its first leader.

00:58:15.130 --> 00:58:17.740
Our new initiative
will examine and assess

00:58:17.740 --> 00:58:22.330
what we have accomplished
and also how race might still

00:58:22.330 --> 00:58:26.590
affect the recruitment, the
retention, and the experiences

00:58:26.590 --> 00:58:30.940
of underrepresented minority
faculty members at MIT.

00:58:30.940 --> 00:58:34.000
The committees the provost
appointed last year

00:58:34.000 --> 00:58:36.460
to address minority recruitment
and retention issues

00:58:36.460 --> 00:58:39.430
will be merged into
this new initiative.

00:58:39.430 --> 00:58:42.520
We're now moving toward the
appointment of a core team.

00:58:42.520 --> 00:58:44.890
That team will consult with--

00:58:44.890 --> 00:58:46.710
and to Elizabeth's reminder--

00:58:46.710 --> 00:58:50.530
will listen to,
listen closely to,

00:58:50.530 --> 00:58:54.770
our minority faculty and
others to define a process--

00:58:54.770 --> 00:58:56.740
we'll define the
process together

00:58:56.740 --> 00:58:59.050
that will lead to a
comprehensive, rigorous, and

00:58:59.050 --> 00:59:01.570
systematic study
of these issues.

00:59:01.570 --> 00:59:03.940
The core team will also
assess the resources

00:59:03.940 --> 00:59:06.970
needed to carry out
this effort thoroughly.

00:59:06.970 --> 00:59:08.470
We believe that
this initiative will

00:59:08.470 --> 00:59:10.870
provide the critical
information needed

00:59:10.870 --> 00:59:13.980
to develop the most
effective ways to strengthen

00:59:13.980 --> 00:59:15.940
the representation
and the career

00:59:15.940 --> 00:59:19.090
experiences of underrepresented
minority faculty members

00:59:19.090 --> 00:59:20.920
at MIT.

00:59:20.920 --> 00:59:24.460
I assure you that the Provost
and I are deeply committed

00:59:24.460 --> 00:59:27.580
to this initiative as a
stimulus for real change

00:59:27.580 --> 00:59:32.320
at the Institute and
as a national example.

00:59:32.320 --> 00:59:34.450
As we move forward to
increase opportunities

00:59:34.450 --> 00:59:36.130
within the faculty,
we will continue

00:59:36.130 --> 00:59:39.310
to do the same for
students and staff.

00:59:39.310 --> 00:59:41.770
Our progress in each
of these domains

00:59:41.770 --> 00:59:44.260
will amplify our
success and the others

00:59:44.260 --> 00:59:49.120
as we build a more diverse
and more inclusive MIT.

00:59:49.120 --> 00:59:53.170
MIT has a reputation as
an excellent employer,

00:59:53.170 --> 00:59:56.200
acknowledged by national awards.

00:59:56.200 --> 01:00:01.300
But we cannot deny that we are
not yet where we want to be

01:00:01.300 --> 01:00:04.210
with respect to the
diversity of our staff,

01:00:04.210 --> 01:00:08.380
nor have we yet laid the paths
to career advancement that can

01:00:08.380 --> 01:00:12.610
inspire the best work and build
the strongest organizations.

01:00:12.610 --> 01:00:16.120
The human resources department
has launched promising efforts

01:00:16.120 --> 01:00:19.090
to reach out to
minority communities.

01:00:19.090 --> 01:00:22.780
And our new vice president for
human resources, Dr. Allison

01:00:22.780 --> 01:00:25.360
Aldon, has made it
clear that this will

01:00:25.360 --> 01:00:28.240
be a central issue for her.

01:00:28.240 --> 01:00:31.450
But recruiting and developing
a more diverse staff

01:00:31.450 --> 01:00:34.720
requires hard work all
across the community

01:00:34.720 --> 01:00:36.640
at all steps of the process.

01:00:36.640 --> 01:00:40.660
Whenever there is a vacancy to
fill, a promotion to be made,

01:00:40.660 --> 01:00:43.120
or a project to
be assigned, I am

01:00:43.120 --> 01:00:48.770
certain that working together,
we can make real progress.

01:00:48.770 --> 01:00:51.550
Our progress has been
greater in student issues,

01:00:51.550 --> 01:00:55.690
as we just heard, thanks to
the activities in Chicago

01:00:55.690 --> 01:00:57.680
and elsewhere.

01:00:57.680 --> 01:01:00.060
But we cannot be complacent.

01:01:00.060 --> 01:01:02.500
And with respect to graduate
students in particular,

01:01:02.500 --> 01:01:05.950
there is still very
much work to do.

01:01:05.950 --> 01:01:11.680
When I first spoke to the MIT
community in August of 2004,

01:01:11.680 --> 01:01:16.960
I said that I wanted MIT to be
the dream of every child who

01:01:16.960 --> 01:01:20.470
wants to make the
world a better place.

01:01:20.470 --> 01:01:24.790
I meant it then,
and I mean it now.

01:01:24.790 --> 01:01:27.490
To achieve that dream,
we must continue

01:01:27.490 --> 01:01:29.260
to dismantle any
impediments that

01:01:29.260 --> 01:01:33.820
may keep exceptional
students from attending MIT.

01:01:33.820 --> 01:01:35.740
Access to educational
opportunity

01:01:35.740 --> 01:01:39.860
is still distributed very
unevenly in our society.

01:01:39.860 --> 01:01:42.700
That's why MIT engages so
many innovative outreach

01:01:42.700 --> 01:01:46.870
programs to draw young people
toward the very best colleges,

01:01:46.870 --> 01:01:50.710
programs such as
STEM, SEED, and MITES.

01:01:50.710 --> 01:01:53.200
These extraordinary programs
prepare young people

01:01:53.200 --> 01:01:56.680
with the skills they
need for success here

01:01:56.680 --> 01:02:00.730
at MIT and at other
great universities.

01:02:00.730 --> 01:02:03.130
Our admissions office has
intensified its efforts

01:02:03.130 --> 01:02:07.180
to recruit the most talented
students from all backgrounds.

01:02:07.180 --> 01:02:10.690
That work has borne fruit,
with this year's freshman

01:02:10.690 --> 01:02:15.340
class the most diverse
in MIT's history.

01:02:15.340 --> 01:02:17.830
Our financial aid
policies open our doors

01:02:17.830 --> 01:02:21.520
to as wide a range of
students as possible.

01:02:21.520 --> 01:02:24.940
As we have for many decades,
we admit undergraduates

01:02:24.940 --> 01:02:28.260
without regard to
their financial need,

01:02:28.260 --> 01:02:35.200
and we award all MIT scholarship
aid on the basis of need alone.

01:02:35.200 --> 01:02:38.650
These policies represent
an institutional investment

01:02:38.650 --> 01:02:42.600
of more than $60
million this year.

01:02:42.600 --> 01:02:45.460
And one of the key objectives
of the campaign for students

01:02:45.460 --> 01:02:47.500
that we launched
earlier in the winter

01:02:47.500 --> 01:02:51.340
is to fully endow
undergraduate financial aid

01:02:51.340 --> 01:02:54.640
to ensure that their
need never, never

01:02:54.640 --> 01:02:58.120
be a decision between
funding financial aid

01:02:58.120 --> 01:03:02.380
and funding other important
institutional activities.

01:03:02.380 --> 01:03:04.240
At the graduate
level, we continue

01:03:04.240 --> 01:03:06.010
to build Institute-wide
recruiting

01:03:06.010 --> 01:03:07.870
efforts, such as
the Converge Weekend

01:03:07.870 --> 01:03:10.120
for prospective students.

01:03:10.120 --> 01:03:12.580
Our role as a leader
in graduate education

01:03:12.580 --> 01:03:16.330
for diverse communities
was recognized last fall

01:03:16.330 --> 01:03:19.030
when MIT became one of
10 universities chosen

01:03:19.030 --> 01:03:22.260
to offer the new
Amgen scholarships,

01:03:22.260 --> 01:03:24.840
a nationwide initiative
that provides opportunities

01:03:24.840 --> 01:03:27.420
for talented
undergraduate students

01:03:27.420 --> 01:03:29.820
to engage in fully
funded summer research

01:03:29.820 --> 01:03:32.430
experiences to
encourage them to pursue

01:03:32.430 --> 01:03:36.840
graduate degrees and,
eventually, careers

01:03:36.840 --> 01:03:39.260
in science and technology.

01:03:39.260 --> 01:03:45.300
And MIT was chosen to host
the program's national office.

01:03:45.300 --> 01:03:48.390
Taking away obstacles for
the Institute's own students

01:03:48.390 --> 01:03:52.800
and for other young people is a
job for all of us gathered here

01:03:52.800 --> 01:03:54.610
today.

01:03:54.610 --> 01:03:59.850
It is a serious but also
a joyful obligation,

01:03:59.850 --> 01:04:03.270
eloquently expressed two
years ago at this breakfast

01:04:03.270 --> 01:04:07.200
by our own student, Sarah
Gonzalez, who's now a senior.

01:04:07.200 --> 01:04:12.520
At that event, Sarah encouraged
us each to do our part.

01:04:12.520 --> 01:04:14.430
This is what she said.

01:04:14.430 --> 01:04:16.050
"I hope that
everyone in this hall

01:04:16.050 --> 01:04:20.580
will reach out and embrace every
opportunity to become a mentor,

01:04:20.580 --> 01:04:24.000
to become a role model, and to
support programs that encourage

01:04:24.000 --> 01:04:26.880
students to accept the challenge
and break through the remaining

01:04:26.880 --> 01:04:31.190
frontiers, professional
and scholarly."

01:04:31.190 --> 01:04:33.330
The truly remarkable
accomplishments

01:04:33.330 --> 01:04:35.790
of our students
and our graduates

01:04:35.790 --> 01:04:41.190
show us why we must hear
and act on Sarah's words.

01:04:45.180 --> 01:04:51.690
Dr. King urges us
to walk together.

01:04:51.690 --> 01:04:56.290
And we have been walking
steadily forward.

01:04:56.290 --> 01:05:00.660
But now, it is time
to pick up the pace.

01:05:00.660 --> 01:05:03.030
We owe that faster pace
to our own community

01:05:03.030 --> 01:05:07.230
of teachers and students,
researchers and staff.

01:05:07.230 --> 01:05:09.540
We owe it to the many
extended communities

01:05:09.540 --> 01:05:14.892
for whom we stand as a beacon
of excellence and aspiration.

01:05:14.892 --> 01:05:16.350
And we owe it to
a world that looks

01:05:16.350 --> 01:05:19.740
to us to bring the very best
thinking to bear on the world's

01:05:19.740 --> 01:05:22.530
toughest problems.

01:05:22.530 --> 01:05:26.430
While each of us must pick
up Sarah Gonzalez's charge,

01:05:26.430 --> 01:05:29.160
we must move ahead
as a community.

01:05:29.160 --> 01:05:35.010
As Dr. King said, we
cannot walk alone.

01:05:35.010 --> 01:05:37.710
And as we walk, we
must make the pledge

01:05:37.710 --> 01:05:39.660
that we shall
always march ahead.

01:05:39.660 --> 01:05:41.190
We cannot turn back.

01:05:43.860 --> 01:05:47.240
Let us walk forward together.

01:05:47.240 --> 01:05:48.230
Thank you.

01:05:48.230 --> 01:05:51.200
[APPLAUSE]

01:06:05.570 --> 01:06:08.510
Now it gives me
truly great pleasure

01:06:08.510 --> 01:06:11.840
to introduce this
year's keynote speaker,

01:06:11.840 --> 01:06:17.600
Ted Childs, Jr. Mr. Childs,
now a world renowned consultant

01:06:17.600 --> 01:06:20.780
specializing in diversity
issues, formerly

01:06:20.780 --> 01:06:24.920
served as the vice president
for Global Workforce Diversity

01:06:24.920 --> 01:06:26.540
at IBM.

01:06:26.540 --> 01:06:30.950
His success in that role at one
of the world's great technology

01:06:30.950 --> 01:06:34.880
companies won him praise as
perhaps the most effective

01:06:34.880 --> 01:06:38.508
diversity executive
on the planet--

01:06:38.508 --> 01:06:39.425
perhaps in the galaxy.

01:06:42.470 --> 01:06:45.440
In his work at IBM, Mr. Childs
stressed the critical role

01:06:45.440 --> 01:06:48.770
of diversity to the
company's business success.

01:06:48.770 --> 01:06:50.900
As he used to say in
speeches to new managers--

01:06:50.900 --> 01:06:52.190
and I love this--

01:06:52.190 --> 01:06:54.350
no matter who you
are, you're going

01:06:54.350 --> 01:06:57.327
to have to work with people
who are different from you.

01:06:57.327 --> 01:06:58.910
You're going to have
to sell to people

01:06:58.910 --> 01:07:01.910
who are different from
you, and buy from people

01:07:01.910 --> 01:07:04.130
who are different from
you, and manage people

01:07:04.130 --> 01:07:06.410
who are different from you.

01:07:06.410 --> 01:07:08.510
His effective leadership
in developing talent

01:07:08.510 --> 01:07:11.720
and strengthening recruiting
and mentoring strategies

01:07:11.720 --> 01:07:15.440
generated enormous gains for
women and minority executives

01:07:15.440 --> 01:07:19.130
at IBM and for the
company itself.

01:07:19.130 --> 01:07:21.500
During a social
service leave from IBM,

01:07:21.500 --> 01:07:23.780
Mr. Childs served as
executive assistant

01:07:23.780 --> 01:07:27.140
to Benjamin L. Hooks,
the executive director

01:07:27.140 --> 01:07:28.983
of the NAACP.

01:07:28.983 --> 01:07:30.650
And he's currently a
member of the board

01:07:30.650 --> 01:07:36.410
of directors of the NAACP Legal
Defense and Education Fund.

01:07:36.410 --> 01:07:39.740
He has been deeply involved
in child care and aging issues

01:07:39.740 --> 01:07:43.130
and also serves as a trustee of
his alma mater, West Virginia

01:07:43.130 --> 01:07:45.530
State University.

01:07:45.530 --> 01:07:47.330
Mr. Childs has been
quoted as saying

01:07:47.330 --> 01:07:50.240
that he is intensely
proud of the gains

01:07:50.240 --> 01:07:52.520
women and members
of minority groups

01:07:52.520 --> 01:07:56.510
made in IBM's executive ranks
during his tenure there.

01:07:56.510 --> 01:07:58.970
He has every right to be.

01:07:58.970 --> 01:08:01.190
His remarks this
morning promise to teach

01:08:01.190 --> 01:08:04.700
all of us how we can
work together to make MIT

01:08:04.700 --> 01:08:07.820
a model of both
diversity and excellence.

01:08:07.820 --> 01:08:10.520
Please join me in extending
MIT's warmest welcome

01:08:10.520 --> 01:08:12.192
to Mr. Ted Childs.

01:08:12.192 --> 01:08:14.647
[APPLAUSE]

01:08:30.293 --> 01:08:31.460
CHILDS: Thank you very much.

01:08:37.100 --> 01:08:46.319
It is always a treat
to be on any college

01:08:46.319 --> 01:08:48.224
campus in our country.

01:08:51.140 --> 01:08:53.359
The joy comes from the
traditions associated

01:08:53.359 --> 01:08:58.090
with college in America,
pursuit of education,

01:08:58.090 --> 01:09:03.090
mental stimulation,
development, spirited debate.

01:09:03.090 --> 01:09:05.010
And when the times
are issue conflicted,

01:09:05.010 --> 01:09:08.850
challenging the system, its
leaders, and our nation's core

01:09:08.850 --> 01:09:11.290
principles and values.

01:09:11.290 --> 01:09:14.840
These are issue
conflicted times.

01:09:14.840 --> 01:09:18.170
And while we have the joy of
being in an academic community,

01:09:18.170 --> 01:09:24.859
I am stressed over the absence
nationally of student activism

01:09:24.859 --> 01:09:29.500
to address what I consider to
be the key issues of our time,

01:09:29.500 --> 01:09:32.529
or to even get a consistent
theme of messages

01:09:32.529 --> 01:09:34.767
that they care.

01:09:34.767 --> 01:09:36.850
I know that this is the
beginning of Black History

01:09:36.850 --> 01:09:37.350
Month.

01:09:37.350 --> 01:09:40.899
It would be easy for me to take
your theme of the convergence

01:09:40.899 --> 01:09:42.700
of diversity and excellence.

01:09:42.700 --> 01:09:45.370
It would be easy to talk
about Black History Month.

01:09:45.370 --> 01:09:51.670
I hope when I'm done, you'll
feel that I did a bit of both.

01:09:56.702 --> 01:09:58.410
I'm interested in this
concept that I saw

01:09:58.410 --> 01:10:00.945
emerge this morning of family.

01:10:00.945 --> 01:10:02.820
I'm teaching a class
down at Bennett College.

01:10:02.820 --> 01:10:05.425
Some of you know Johnnetta
Cole, the president of Bennett.

01:10:05.425 --> 01:10:07.050
This is her last
semester, and I agreed

01:10:07.050 --> 01:10:09.000
to teach a seminar there.

01:10:09.000 --> 01:10:12.570
And each week, the
students have gotten

01:10:12.570 --> 01:10:16.780
accustomed to me saying to them,
you can't make this stuff up.

01:10:16.780 --> 01:10:18.450
I come in each week
with two or three

01:10:18.450 --> 01:10:20.460
news stories of
the last day or so

01:10:20.460 --> 01:10:23.890
that are relevant to
what I'm talking about.

01:10:23.890 --> 01:10:30.800
And this week, we had
something happen that I

01:10:30.800 --> 01:10:36.530
thought was quite provocative.

01:10:36.530 --> 01:10:40.540
Across town here, you had
a new president installed.

01:10:40.540 --> 01:10:45.280
And the New York Times did
quite a provocative piece on her

01:10:45.280 --> 01:10:51.130
on Monday, and I read it and
highlighted a couple of things.

01:10:51.130 --> 01:10:53.530
Recalling her coming
of age as the only girl

01:10:53.530 --> 01:10:58.060
in a privileged tradition bound
family in Virginia, Virginia

01:10:58.060 --> 01:11:03.780
horse country specifically,
Drew Gilpin Faust

01:11:03.780 --> 01:11:06.870
has often spoken of her
continued confrontation

01:11:06.870 --> 01:11:10.410
with her mother about the
requirements of which she

01:11:10.410 --> 01:11:12.750
usually called femininity.

01:11:12.750 --> 01:11:14.535
Her mother Catherine,
she has said,

01:11:14.535 --> 01:11:18.510
told her repeatedly, it's
a man's world, sweetie,

01:11:18.510 --> 01:11:20.970
and the sooner you learn
that, the better you will be.

01:11:23.580 --> 01:11:25.770
She's written, frankly,
of the community

01:11:25.770 --> 01:11:30.570
of rigid racial segregation that
she and her brothers grew up in

01:11:30.570 --> 01:11:35.133
and how it formed her as
a rebellious daughter who

01:11:35.133 --> 01:11:36.800
would go on to march
in the civil rights

01:11:36.800 --> 01:11:39.080
protests of the
'60s and to become

01:11:39.080 --> 01:11:41.670
an historian of the region.

01:11:41.670 --> 01:11:44.350
Race was not much
discussed in her family.

01:11:44.350 --> 01:11:47.940
She wrote in an article
printed in Harvard Magazine,

01:11:47.940 --> 01:11:50.250
"I live in a world where
social arrangements were

01:11:50.250 --> 01:11:55.330
taken for granted and
assumed to be timeless.

01:11:55.330 --> 01:11:58.220
A child's obligation was
to learn these usages,

01:11:58.220 --> 01:12:00.690
not to question them.

01:12:00.690 --> 01:12:06.330
The complexities of racial
deportment were of a piece

01:12:06.330 --> 01:12:12.360
with learning manners
and etiquette.

01:12:12.360 --> 01:12:14.730
There were formalized
ways of organizing

01:12:14.730 --> 01:12:19.680
almost every aspect of human
relationships and interactions.

01:12:19.680 --> 01:12:23.670
How you placed your fork
and knife on the plate,

01:12:23.670 --> 01:12:25.500
when you had finished
eating, what you

01:12:25.500 --> 01:12:29.040
did with the finger bowl,
who walked through a door

01:12:29.040 --> 01:12:33.640
first, whose name was spoken
first in an introduction, how

01:12:33.640 --> 01:12:38.890
others were dressed, black
adults with just a first name,

01:12:38.890 --> 01:12:42.700
whites as Mr. or Mrs.,
whose hand you shook

01:12:42.700 --> 01:12:45.873
and whose you didn't, who
ate in the dining room

01:12:45.873 --> 01:12:47.040
and who ate in the kitchen."

01:12:51.162 --> 01:12:54.090
I have heard stories about that.

01:12:54.090 --> 01:12:58.060
That is the first time I
have ever seen it in writing,

01:12:58.060 --> 01:13:00.180
and it's quite riveting.

01:13:02.700 --> 01:13:06.660
I was interested this
morning because I

01:13:06.660 --> 01:13:10.380
had that news story on
Monday, and yesterday, we

01:13:10.380 --> 01:13:14.670
had a new story because one of
the more enlightened members

01:13:14.670 --> 01:13:19.200
of our athletic establishment,
Mr. Timothy Hardaway,

01:13:19.200 --> 01:13:24.390
said in a radio interview
about how much he hated gays,

01:13:24.390 --> 01:13:28.720
didn't want to be
on a team with one.

01:13:28.720 --> 01:13:30.950
Now he's a retired
player from the NBA.

01:13:30.950 --> 01:13:33.920
He was scheduled to participate
in the all-star events

01:13:33.920 --> 01:13:35.060
this weekend.

01:13:35.060 --> 01:13:37.550
And I was very proud that
Commissioner David Stern

01:13:37.550 --> 01:13:39.830
stepped up to him
immediately and said,

01:13:39.830 --> 01:13:43.820
you will not participate in
any NBA-related activity.

01:13:43.820 --> 01:13:47.436
Your views are not compatible
with the views of the NBA.

01:13:47.436 --> 01:13:49.876
[APPLAUSE]

01:13:56.220 --> 01:14:00.690
Commissioner Stern's
response was, in my view,

01:14:00.690 --> 01:14:05.790
compatible with the requirements
of leadership today.

01:14:05.790 --> 01:14:08.300
When I left IBM in
August, I started

01:14:08.300 --> 01:14:10.850
my own business,
Ted Childs, LLC,

01:14:10.850 --> 01:14:14.220
and I needed
somebody to help me.

01:14:14.220 --> 01:14:17.080
So I knew this young lady.

01:14:17.080 --> 01:14:21.450
She had an undergraduate degree
from Florida A&amp;M in sociology.

01:14:21.450 --> 01:14:24.600
She has a master's
degree in sociology

01:14:24.600 --> 01:14:26.920
from University of Texas.

01:14:26.920 --> 01:14:29.700
She has a master's degree
in human resource management

01:14:29.700 --> 01:14:32.330
from Rutgers.

01:14:32.330 --> 01:14:37.080
And I said to her, I paid
for all those tuition bills.

01:14:37.080 --> 01:14:41.667
Why don't you come join
me in this business?

01:14:41.667 --> 01:14:42.750
And I tell you that story.

01:14:42.750 --> 01:14:44.260
I did not plan to
talk about that,

01:14:44.260 --> 01:14:46.480
but I saw something
this morning.

01:14:46.480 --> 01:14:52.475
No one that I heard commented
that very wonderful set

01:14:52.475 --> 01:14:54.350
of remarks that we heard
from Elizabeth Clay.

01:14:54.350 --> 01:14:56.990
She's the daughter of
Phillip Clay who was here.

01:14:56.990 --> 01:14:59.020
You can grow your own talent.

01:14:59.020 --> 01:15:01.587
We didn't talk about--

01:15:01.587 --> 01:15:02.170
well, hold on.

01:15:02.170 --> 01:15:03.400
Hold on.

01:15:03.400 --> 01:15:06.750
We didn't talk about
Austin and Michelle Harton.

01:15:06.750 --> 01:15:08.500
They've got two daughters
who are students

01:15:08.500 --> 01:15:11.620
at MIT seated over there.

01:15:11.620 --> 01:15:15.030
My colleague, [? Aida ?]
[? Saibot ?] from EMC is here.

01:15:15.030 --> 01:15:17.260
[? Aida ?] is heads
up diversity for EMC,

01:15:17.260 --> 01:15:19.240
but she's an
engineer by training,

01:15:19.240 --> 01:15:21.770
and her daughter
graduated with a 3.8

01:15:21.770 --> 01:15:24.670
in engineering from
Penn State in December.

01:15:24.670 --> 01:15:30.790
It seems that you can truly grow
your own talent if you plant

01:15:30.790 --> 01:15:33.490
the seed and water that plant.

01:15:33.490 --> 01:15:36.760
So I commend all of
you for your parenting,

01:15:36.760 --> 01:15:38.807
and I join you in that.

01:15:38.807 --> 01:15:41.192
[APPLAUSE]

01:15:46.920 --> 01:15:48.960
If you follow the
thread of my comments,

01:15:48.960 --> 01:15:51.390
you will find me
in the neighborhood

01:15:51.390 --> 01:15:55.395
of workforce diversity.

01:15:58.713 --> 01:16:00.880
And if I don't touch what
you want me to talk about,

01:16:00.880 --> 01:16:03.337
if we have time,
I'll take it in Q&amp;A.

01:16:03.337 --> 01:16:05.170
My colleagues at the
American Program Bureau

01:16:05.170 --> 01:16:07.587
that coordinate my speeches
say that they're wary about me

01:16:07.587 --> 01:16:10.960
because I write my own
speeches, one for each audience.

01:16:10.960 --> 01:16:14.720
They say, you know, you
should have a stump speech.

01:16:14.720 --> 01:16:18.020
And I tell them, subjects may
be the same, but the issues

01:16:18.020 --> 01:16:21.260
and the times, they change.

01:16:21.260 --> 01:16:24.500
Lyndon Johnson wrote that
you will find meaning only

01:16:24.500 --> 01:16:26.540
by sharing in the
responsibilities, the dangers,

01:16:26.540 --> 01:16:29.750
and the passions of your time.

01:16:29.750 --> 01:16:34.750
In the '60s, in my college
classes, the class of '67,

01:16:34.750 --> 01:16:37.280
we were engaged in issues.

01:16:37.280 --> 01:16:40.970
For examples, lunch
counter sit-ins

01:16:40.970 --> 01:16:42.960
in Greensboro, North Carolina.

01:16:42.960 --> 01:16:46.690
In February of 1964, a young
black man from North Carolina,

01:16:46.690 --> 01:16:51.380
A&amp;T University, an
historically black college,

01:16:51.380 --> 01:16:55.620
sat at a lunch counter at
a Woolworths Department

01:16:55.620 --> 01:16:57.300
Store in Greensboro.

01:16:57.300 --> 01:16:59.630
They were refused
service that day.

01:16:59.630 --> 01:17:02.010
They remained in their
seats until closing time,

01:17:02.010 --> 01:17:05.610
and the sit-in
movement was born.

01:17:05.610 --> 01:17:08.560
The protests spread to 54
cities and nine states.

01:17:08.560 --> 01:17:10.680
And over a two-month
period, the economic impact

01:17:10.680 --> 01:17:13.510
was as pronounced as
the social impact,

01:17:13.510 --> 01:17:16.600
so much so that
on July 25, 1960,

01:17:16.600 --> 01:17:19.320
the first black person
ate a meal sitting down

01:17:19.320 --> 01:17:21.920
at that same Woolworths.

01:17:21.920 --> 01:17:27.330
And that lunch counter is now
on display at the Smithsonian.

01:17:27.330 --> 01:17:32.070
Later in the '60s, Michael
Schwerner, a Cornell graduate

01:17:32.070 --> 01:17:36.240
student, Cornell graduate, a
social worker in Manhattan,

01:17:36.240 --> 01:17:39.210
James Chaney, a young
black man from Mississippi,

01:17:39.210 --> 01:17:42.900
Andrew Goodman, a Queens
College sophomore,

01:17:42.900 --> 01:17:46.960
all involved in a voting
rights activity in Mississippi,

01:17:46.960 --> 01:17:50.830
two white and one
black all killed

01:17:50.830 --> 01:17:53.650
in pursuit of their mission.

01:17:53.650 --> 01:17:56.720
1968, the Democratic Convention.

01:17:56.720 --> 01:17:59.510
The events that occurred at
the Democratic Convention

01:17:59.510 --> 01:18:02.240
in Chicago in 1968 brought
about several changes

01:18:02.240 --> 01:18:04.410
in politics and society.

01:18:04.410 --> 01:18:06.870
It was a defining moment
for the youth in America

01:18:06.870 --> 01:18:09.988
that were protesting
the war in Vietnam.

01:18:09.988 --> 01:18:11.780
And young people saw
that they could really

01:18:11.780 --> 01:18:14.570
make a difference in
confronting the establishment.

01:18:14.570 --> 01:18:17.810
The anti-war
movement really came

01:18:17.810 --> 01:18:19.220
to have a new
level of prominence

01:18:19.220 --> 01:18:20.728
at the Chicago convention.

01:18:20.728 --> 01:18:23.270
And it could be argued that it
laid the groundwork for troops

01:18:23.270 --> 01:18:28.040
to be withdrawn from
Vietnam in April of 1975.

01:18:28.040 --> 01:18:32.890
And I was reminded when
you talked about you're

01:18:32.890 --> 01:18:35.350
a member of the
Chicago Seven, there's

01:18:35.350 --> 01:18:38.110
a Chicago Eight
from that convention

01:18:38.110 --> 01:18:41.020
that had another impact.

01:18:41.020 --> 01:18:44.530
But they would be proud of you.

01:18:44.530 --> 01:18:46.190
West Virginia State
College, a college

01:18:46.190 --> 01:18:48.670
that was a member of the
historically black college

01:18:48.670 --> 01:18:50.860
and university network.

01:18:50.860 --> 01:18:53.052
Until the 1954 Supreme
Court decision,

01:18:53.052 --> 01:18:54.760
it was against the
law for white students

01:18:54.760 --> 01:18:56.590
to attend that college.

01:18:56.590 --> 01:19:00.400
I arrived on that campus
in September of 1963.

01:19:00.400 --> 01:19:03.010
I arrived on a campus
that was 80% white.

01:19:03.010 --> 01:19:05.470
The dormitories were 90% black.

01:19:05.470 --> 01:19:07.630
In the minds of black
America, West Virginia State

01:19:07.630 --> 01:19:09.720
was still a black college.

01:19:09.720 --> 01:19:12.067
In the minds of white
West Virginians,

01:19:12.067 --> 01:19:14.400
here was a school where they
could get a good education,

01:19:14.400 --> 01:19:17.890
go to school in the day
time, and go home at night.

01:19:17.890 --> 01:19:21.070
1966 was our senior year
and the 75th anniversary

01:19:21.070 --> 01:19:22.790
of the college.

01:19:22.790 --> 01:19:27.135
Student body president, a white
student named Steve Bragg,

01:19:27.135 --> 01:19:28.510
was one of the
few white students

01:19:28.510 --> 01:19:31.662
who lived in the dorms, and we
had, over time, become friends.

01:19:31.662 --> 01:19:33.370
Steve asked me to be
the social committee

01:19:33.370 --> 01:19:37.840
chair for our senior year, the
major event being homecoming.

01:19:37.840 --> 01:19:40.000
I agreed to accept
the role if he

01:19:40.000 --> 01:19:42.910
agreed that our primary
goal at homecoming

01:19:42.910 --> 01:19:45.490
would be to
integrate our campus.

01:19:45.490 --> 01:19:48.100
From 1954 to 1965,
there had never

01:19:48.100 --> 01:19:51.770
been more than six white
couples come to homecoming,

01:19:51.770 --> 01:19:53.140
to the dance.

01:19:53.140 --> 01:19:55.360
That year, we hired a
very popular white band,

01:19:55.360 --> 01:19:59.850
The Esquires, and a
popular black band, Martha

01:19:59.850 --> 01:20:01.265
and the Vandellas.

01:20:01.265 --> 01:20:04.240
[APPLAUSE]

01:20:04.240 --> 01:20:06.220
The black students
had fun, and they

01:20:06.220 --> 01:20:10.688
were joined by 600 of
their white colleagues.

01:20:10.688 --> 01:20:11.980
We had a moment of integration.

01:20:11.980 --> 01:20:14.320
We stood up to the
patterns of our time

01:20:14.320 --> 01:20:16.900
and said we are one
student body and would

01:20:16.900 --> 01:20:19.150
be consistent with the motto
of our college, a living

01:20:19.150 --> 01:20:21.740
laboratory of human relations.

01:20:21.740 --> 01:20:23.900
Managing that event
became a pivotal event

01:20:23.900 --> 01:20:29.080
in determining the professional
course my life would take.

01:20:29.080 --> 01:20:31.810
Examples of student activism,
challenging, standing up,

01:20:31.810 --> 01:20:34.480
fighting for principles
and values that we believed

01:20:34.480 --> 01:20:37.270
were right and consistent
with the founding principles

01:20:37.270 --> 01:20:38.980
of our nation.

01:20:38.980 --> 01:20:41.650
The '60s were also the
time of corporate awakening

01:20:41.650 --> 01:20:43.270
in the United States.

01:20:43.270 --> 01:20:45.220
We're coming off
a 30-year period

01:20:45.220 --> 01:20:48.310
of visible examples of
challenge, social questioning,

01:20:48.310 --> 01:20:52.600
sometimes violent
behavior and change.

01:20:52.600 --> 01:20:55.150
Nazi Germany and the
Holocaust gave us

01:20:55.150 --> 01:20:58.710
real insight into the depth
of bigotry and hatred.

01:20:58.710 --> 01:21:01.230
The attack on Pearl Harbor
shook our nation to its core,

01:21:01.230 --> 01:21:04.530
but also sparked a reaction
that we would later regret--

01:21:04.530 --> 01:21:07.590
the internment of our
Japanese citizens.

01:21:07.590 --> 01:21:09.808
The world saw the debate
about the war lead

01:21:09.808 --> 01:21:11.850
to the founding of the
United Nations in the hope

01:21:11.850 --> 01:21:15.000
that it would be a forum to
avoid conflict, a place where

01:21:15.000 --> 01:21:17.940
we could talk, not
before shooting,

01:21:17.940 --> 01:21:20.440
but in place of shooting.

01:21:20.440 --> 01:21:22.780
In the United
States 60 years ago,

01:21:22.780 --> 01:21:27.330
April 15, 1947, Jackie
Robinson integrated

01:21:27.330 --> 01:21:28.635
our nation's pastime, baseball.

01:21:31.630 --> 01:21:35.500
And later, on July 26,
1948, President Harry Truman

01:21:35.500 --> 01:21:38.440
issued Executive Order
9981, abolishing segregation

01:21:38.440 --> 01:21:41.440
in the armed forces and
ordering full integration

01:21:41.440 --> 01:21:45.790
of all the services.

01:21:45.790 --> 01:21:49.340
While World War
II had concluded,

01:21:49.340 --> 01:21:51.380
the Korean War shortly followed.

01:21:51.380 --> 01:21:53.880
And black and white
soldiers actually

01:21:53.880 --> 01:21:56.700
got to fight and die together.

01:21:56.700 --> 01:22:00.165
Now, I'm going to sprinkle
my remarks with some sidebar

01:22:00.165 --> 01:22:00.665
stories.

01:22:03.180 --> 01:22:07.830
Tabitha, when you talked
about Dr. King having

01:22:07.830 --> 01:22:09.800
two segments to his
civil right activity,

01:22:09.800 --> 01:22:12.800
I penciled some notes here.

01:22:12.800 --> 01:22:15.080
Because a friend of
mine, Dr. Larry Spruill,

01:22:15.080 --> 01:22:20.860
did his doctoral dissertation
on the civil rights movement

01:22:20.860 --> 01:22:22.457
and the media.

01:22:22.457 --> 01:22:25.040
And when I talked to Larry about
it and read his dissertation,

01:22:25.040 --> 01:22:26.707
I learned something
that I did not know,

01:22:26.707 --> 01:22:30.470
and that was that the media
played an important role

01:22:30.470 --> 01:22:34.250
in the civil rights movement,
but Dr. King was a strategist.

01:22:38.090 --> 01:22:41.030
The march to Pettus
Bridge and what

01:22:41.030 --> 01:22:47.720
happened surrounding that
was orchestrated by him.

01:22:47.720 --> 01:22:51.140
He said, in a
strategy meeting, we

01:22:51.140 --> 01:22:57.110
will continue to be beaten,
but not in the dark of night,

01:22:57.110 --> 01:23:00.800
but only in the
full light of day.

01:23:00.800 --> 01:23:05.940
And he planned that march
so that the media could tell

01:23:05.940 --> 01:23:09.810
the story, because he
picked that place because he

01:23:09.810 --> 01:23:14.540
knew that they could provoke
Bull Connor to attack them

01:23:14.540 --> 01:23:17.530
and that America would see
what they were subjected.

01:23:17.530 --> 01:23:21.380
I got to read what happened when
they talked to the newspaper

01:23:21.380 --> 01:23:23.900
and magazine reporters and
saw one of the magazine

01:23:23.900 --> 01:23:26.420
reporters talked about the
people there, the police.

01:23:26.420 --> 01:23:28.250
They knew who they were.

01:23:28.250 --> 01:23:30.770
They did not want them
showing up with their cameras,

01:23:30.770 --> 01:23:34.550
and how the photographers
trained themselves

01:23:34.550 --> 01:23:38.380
to hide their cameras
under their coats

01:23:38.380 --> 01:23:42.820
strapped to their bodies so that
they could bring the coat up,

01:23:42.820 --> 01:23:45.940
flash the camera
at the scene, snap,

01:23:45.940 --> 01:23:48.840
put their coat down again,
so that the police wouldn't

01:23:48.840 --> 01:23:50.400
know that they were there.

01:23:50.400 --> 01:23:53.650
And those were the
pictures that America saw

01:23:53.650 --> 01:23:56.980
that helped America understand.

01:23:56.980 --> 01:24:01.270
Now, three weeks ago, I had one
of those personal experiences

01:24:01.270 --> 01:24:03.940
that kind of live with you.

01:24:03.940 --> 01:24:08.590
I was on a plane headed
to Austin, Texas.

01:24:08.590 --> 01:24:12.940
And the flight attendant came
by to take our meal orders.

01:24:12.940 --> 01:24:17.380
And she addressed the man seated
next to me as Mr. Greenberg.

01:24:17.380 --> 01:24:22.140
And I didn't pay
any attention to it,

01:24:22.140 --> 01:24:25.290
but then we were
still at the gate,

01:24:25.290 --> 01:24:29.710
and he took a cell phone call.

01:24:29.710 --> 01:24:33.580
And that prompted me, when
I heard his conversation,

01:24:33.580 --> 01:24:36.050
to ask him a question.

01:24:36.050 --> 01:24:39.230
I said, look, I heard the
flight attendant address you,

01:24:39.230 --> 01:24:44.720
and I couldn't help
overhearing your conversation.

01:24:44.720 --> 01:24:46.213
Your name is Greenberg.

01:24:49.051 --> 01:24:54.800
Are you any relation
to Hank Greenberg,

01:24:54.800 --> 01:24:57.800
the man who played baseball?

01:24:57.800 --> 01:25:00.950
And he said, yes, I'm his son.

01:25:00.950 --> 01:25:06.320
And I said, 58 home runs, 1938.

01:25:06.320 --> 01:25:11.125
And he said, 1937.

01:25:11.125 --> 01:25:21.980
Now, I have a hallway in my home
named Jackie Robinson Parkway.

01:25:24.920 --> 01:25:27.430
And I have a street
sign that I got

01:25:27.430 --> 01:25:29.180
that I bought at an
auction, that it hangs

01:25:29.180 --> 01:25:31.250
at the top of that hallway.

01:25:31.250 --> 01:25:34.320
I grew up in Massachusetts.

01:25:34.320 --> 01:25:37.220
I was born and raised
in Springfield.

01:25:37.220 --> 01:25:39.680
My dad loved baseball
and in another time,

01:25:39.680 --> 01:25:43.010
might have been able
to play professionally.

01:25:43.010 --> 01:25:46.687
He would not bring
me to Fenway Park

01:25:46.687 --> 01:25:48.270
because the Red Sox
were the last team

01:25:48.270 --> 01:25:49.260
to have black players--

01:25:49.260 --> 01:25:52.020
Pumpsie Green in 1959.

01:25:52.020 --> 01:25:53.890
But he took me to Ebbets Field.

01:25:53.890 --> 01:25:57.040
I saw Jackie play, and Roy
Campanella and Don Newcombe.

01:25:57.040 --> 01:25:59.360
They were my heroes.

01:25:59.360 --> 01:26:03.370
But for three hours,
Stephen Greenberg and I

01:26:03.370 --> 01:26:06.760
had a riveting discussion.

01:26:06.760 --> 01:26:09.653
And he sent me some
artifacts about his dad.

01:26:09.653 --> 01:26:11.070
He is sending me
pictures that are

01:26:11.070 --> 01:26:13.320
going to hang on
Jackie Robinson Parkway

01:26:13.320 --> 01:26:15.390
with some pictures of Jackie.

01:26:15.390 --> 01:26:23.780
But when we think about what
we celebrate, years later,

01:26:23.780 --> 01:26:26.510
Elden Auker, the
Detroit pitcher,

01:26:26.510 --> 01:26:30.770
recalled during the game,
somebody from the White Sox

01:26:30.770 --> 01:26:35.105
dugout yelled out that Hank was
a yellow Jew son of a bitch.

01:26:38.120 --> 01:26:42.670
When the ball game was over,
Hank took off his spikes,

01:26:42.670 --> 01:26:45.550
and he walked across the
way and opened the door.

01:26:45.550 --> 01:26:48.280
He walked right into
the White Sox clubhouse,

01:26:48.280 --> 01:26:52.690
which was just across the aisle
from our clubhouse, and said,

01:26:52.690 --> 01:26:55.720
the guy that called me a yellow
son of a bitch, get on his feet

01:26:55.720 --> 01:27:00.810
and come out here and
call it to my face.

01:27:00.810 --> 01:27:02.730
Not a guy moved.

01:27:02.730 --> 01:27:05.460
He was damn lucky because
Hank would have killed him.

01:27:05.460 --> 01:27:07.700
Hank was a tough guy.

01:27:07.700 --> 01:27:10.250
Birdie Tebbetts, a young
reserve catcher on the Tigers

01:27:10.250 --> 01:27:13.070
and a Detroit teammate of
Hank's for seven seasons,

01:27:13.070 --> 01:27:15.260
also remembered that incident.

01:27:15.260 --> 01:27:17.480
Hank walked into the White
Sox locker room and said,

01:27:17.480 --> 01:27:19.700
I don't know who called
me a yellow Jew bastard,

01:27:19.700 --> 01:27:24.940
but whoever it was, stand
up and say it to my face.

01:27:24.940 --> 01:27:27.160
Tebbetts recalled
years later, there

01:27:27.160 --> 01:27:29.020
was nobody in the
history of the game who

01:27:29.020 --> 01:27:34.260
took more abuse than Greenberg
unless it was Jackie.

01:27:34.260 --> 01:27:38.140
I was there with Hank when it
was happening, and I heard it.

01:27:38.140 --> 01:27:40.720
However, Hank was
not only equal to it,

01:27:40.720 --> 01:27:44.080
he was superior to most of the
people who were yelling at him.

01:27:44.080 --> 01:27:47.540
And in the case of Jackie,
Jackie had no place

01:27:47.540 --> 01:27:49.910
to go after a ball
game, and Greenberg

01:27:49.910 --> 01:27:52.910
could go anyplace in the world.

01:27:52.910 --> 01:27:56.430
Greenberg had to bear the
terrible burden on the field.

01:27:56.430 --> 01:27:59.590
Jackie had to bear
it all of his life.

01:27:59.590 --> 01:28:01.850
I wasn't in the National
League with Jackie,

01:28:01.850 --> 01:28:08.078
but I was with Hank, and Hank
consistently took more abuse.

01:28:08.078 --> 01:28:09.620
In those days, there
was an awful lot

01:28:09.620 --> 01:28:11.660
of what we called jockeying.

01:28:11.660 --> 01:28:13.850
There's no room for it
now because the organists

01:28:13.850 --> 01:28:16.680
have taken over.

01:28:16.680 --> 01:28:18.420
It was between innings.

01:28:18.420 --> 01:28:20.940
We got our feelings across to
everybody-- umpires, hitters,

01:28:20.940 --> 01:28:21.930
pitchers.

01:28:21.930 --> 01:28:23.423
It was a continuous thing.

01:28:23.423 --> 01:28:24.840
There were bench
jockeys who would

01:28:24.840 --> 01:28:29.320
get on the edge of the bench
and yell obscenities, or jokes,

01:28:29.320 --> 01:28:31.840
or something about a
guy's personal life.

01:28:31.840 --> 01:28:34.690
And you'd hear it
out in the stands.

01:28:34.690 --> 01:28:39.840
To Greenberg, you'd hear, Jew
bastard or kike son of a bitch.

01:28:39.840 --> 01:28:42.630
Nobody else could have withstood
the foul invectives that

01:28:42.630 --> 01:28:48.230
were directed toward Greenberg,
and he had to eat them.

01:28:48.230 --> 01:28:50.792
Or else, he would
be out of a game.

01:28:50.792 --> 01:28:52.250
Tebbetts recalled
another time when

01:28:52.250 --> 01:28:54.530
the Yankees were writing
Greenberg from the bench

01:28:54.530 --> 01:28:55.640
during a game.

01:28:55.640 --> 01:28:57.980
Hank walked over to
the Yankee bench,

01:28:57.980 --> 01:29:01.910
said Tebbetts, and
challenged the entire team.

01:29:01.910 --> 01:29:04.620
Nobody said a word.

01:29:04.620 --> 01:29:06.540
Ben Chapman, who played
on several teams,

01:29:06.540 --> 01:29:08.400
was considered one
of the toughest bench

01:29:08.400 --> 01:29:10.120
jockeys in baseball.

01:29:10.120 --> 01:29:12.090
And he was tough on Greenberg.

01:29:12.090 --> 01:29:14.310
He was also rough on
Jackie, after Jackie

01:29:14.310 --> 01:29:16.390
broke into the National League.

01:29:16.390 --> 01:29:18.565
Chapman was then the
manager of the Phillies.

01:29:18.565 --> 01:29:20.190
It was Chapman who
was supposed to have

01:29:20.190 --> 01:29:25.100
thrown a black cat on the
field to try to shame Robinson.

01:29:25.100 --> 01:29:28.507
And Robinson's most difficult
moment, he would say later,

01:29:28.507 --> 01:29:30.590
was when he swallowed his
pride in his rookie year

01:29:30.590 --> 01:29:32.480
and buried the
hatchet with Chapman

01:29:32.480 --> 01:29:34.314
and posed for a photograph.

01:29:38.990 --> 01:29:41.830
A short column in the
New York Times on May 18

01:29:41.830 --> 01:29:44.660
where the headline,
"Hank Greenberg a

01:29:44.660 --> 01:29:48.918
Hero to Dodgers Negro Star."

01:29:48.918 --> 01:29:50.960
The story read, "Jackie
Robinson, the first Negro

01:29:50.960 --> 01:29:55.610
player, has picked the diamond
hero, the rival first baseman

01:29:55.610 --> 01:29:57.950
Hank Greenberg of the
Pittsburgh Pirates.

01:29:57.950 --> 01:29:59.540
Here's why.

01:29:59.540 --> 01:30:02.810
Robinson and Greenberg collided
in a play at first base

01:30:02.810 --> 01:30:05.910
during the current
Dodger-Pirates series.

01:30:05.910 --> 01:30:08.750
The next time Jackie
came down to the set,

01:30:08.750 --> 01:30:13.010
Hank said, I forgot to ask you
if you were hurt on that play.

01:30:13.010 --> 01:30:14.510
Assured that Robinson
was unharmed,

01:30:14.510 --> 01:30:17.540
Greenberg said,
Jackie, stick in there.

01:30:17.540 --> 01:30:20.470
You're doing fine.

01:30:20.470 --> 01:30:22.580
Keep your chin up.

01:30:22.580 --> 01:30:24.500
This encouragement from
an established star

01:30:24.500 --> 01:30:26.780
heartened Robinson who
has been the subject

01:30:26.780 --> 01:30:30.240
of racial treatment.

01:30:30.240 --> 01:30:32.370
Jackie came into Pittsburgh
on a Friday afternoon,

01:30:32.370 --> 01:30:34.980
and the place was jammed.

01:30:34.980 --> 01:30:37.960
We were in last place, and the
Dodgers were in first place.

01:30:37.960 --> 01:30:40.230
Our southern ball players,
a bunch of bench jockeys,

01:30:40.230 --> 01:30:44.580
kept yelling to
Jackie, hey, coal mine.

01:30:44.580 --> 01:30:45.990
Hey, coal mine.

01:30:45.990 --> 01:30:48.030
Hey, you black coal mine.

01:30:48.030 --> 01:30:49.170
We're going to get you.

01:30:49.170 --> 01:30:51.790
You ain't going to
play no baseball here.

01:30:51.790 --> 01:30:53.730
Jackie paid them no mind.

01:30:53.730 --> 01:30:56.670
He got on base and
started dancing.

01:30:56.670 --> 01:30:57.960
It was beautiful to watch.

01:30:57.960 --> 01:31:00.267
I couldn't help but admire him.

01:31:00.267 --> 01:31:02.100
Anyway, we were in last
place and these guys

01:31:02.100 --> 01:31:04.260
were calling a guy on a
first place team names.

01:31:07.290 --> 01:31:12.610
The last thing that
stuck out was later on,

01:31:12.610 --> 01:31:15.340
Hank Greenberg became
the general manager

01:31:15.340 --> 01:31:17.770
of the Cleveland Indians.

01:31:17.770 --> 01:31:25.210
And he traveled with the players
on their last field trip.

01:31:28.733 --> 01:31:30.150
We were leading
the league, and it

01:31:30.150 --> 01:31:32.490
looked like we were
going to win the pennant.

01:31:32.490 --> 01:31:34.860
From Washington, we went
to Baltimore by bus.

01:31:34.860 --> 01:31:39.090
When we arrived at
the Baltimore hotel,

01:31:39.090 --> 01:31:41.670
all the players rushed
to get off the bus.

01:31:41.670 --> 01:31:43.680
I waited until all the
players had left the bus,

01:31:43.680 --> 01:31:47.540
and then I found myself standing
there with five players.

01:31:47.540 --> 01:31:50.402
They were not going to the
hotel because they were black.

01:31:50.402 --> 01:31:52.610
It hadn't occurred to me
that these players would not

01:31:52.610 --> 01:31:54.260
be admitted to the hotel.

01:31:54.260 --> 01:31:56.480
I asked them, what
do you guys do?

01:31:56.480 --> 01:31:58.940
They said, we wait for the taxi.

01:31:58.940 --> 01:32:00.650
The taxis take us
to different homes,

01:32:00.650 --> 01:32:01.733
and we stay with families.

01:32:05.420 --> 01:32:07.610
And I thought to myself,
this is terrible.

01:32:07.610 --> 01:32:11.070
Here, these fellows are
a vital part of the team,

01:32:11.070 --> 01:32:13.950
contributing to our success,
and they have to be segregated

01:32:13.950 --> 01:32:17.080
and they're not permitted
to stay in the hotel.

01:32:17.080 --> 01:32:20.340
And I said to Spud Goldstein,
our traveling secretary,

01:32:20.340 --> 01:32:22.620
this is not going on any longer.

01:32:22.620 --> 01:32:25.980
For next season, I want to
write a letter to every hotel

01:32:25.980 --> 01:32:29.600
before the season
opens and tell them

01:32:29.600 --> 01:32:32.150
that we will not
send our team there

01:32:32.150 --> 01:32:34.850
unless everyone on the team
is accepted and treated

01:32:34.850 --> 01:32:37.577
as a guest with the
same equal rights.

01:32:37.577 --> 01:32:39.160
I found that blacks
were not permitted

01:32:39.160 --> 01:32:41.887
in the hotels in St. Louis,
Baltimore, and Washington.

01:32:41.887 --> 01:32:43.720
So the next year, we
wrote to all the hotels

01:32:43.720 --> 01:32:46.900
and explained that if they
wanted the Cleveland Indians'

01:32:46.900 --> 01:32:51.550
business, they would take all
of our players or none of them.

01:32:51.550 --> 01:32:58.110
Now, all of the hotels
said, bring your players."

01:32:58.110 --> 01:33:03.630
Now, to me, one of the riveting
things about my business

01:33:03.630 --> 01:33:05.953
is I have a saying that
I have trademarked.

01:33:05.953 --> 01:33:07.870
I don't care who you
hate, you don't hate them

01:33:07.870 --> 01:33:08.912
more than you love money.

01:33:18.649 --> 01:33:24.865
On September 21, 1953,
Tom Watson, Jr, then

01:33:24.865 --> 01:33:26.240
the chief executive
at IBM, wrote

01:33:26.240 --> 01:33:28.370
what we think was America's
first equal opportunity policy

01:33:28.370 --> 01:33:29.015
letter.

01:33:29.015 --> 01:33:33.160
Now I retired from IBM
last August after 39 years,

01:33:33.160 --> 01:33:37.400
but I worked for a new-- every
CEO, except Tom, Sr, his dad.

01:33:37.400 --> 01:33:41.710
And I interviewed Tom,
Jr, in the early '90s.

01:33:41.710 --> 01:33:45.180
And I asked him, why did
you write that letter?

01:33:45.180 --> 01:33:49.140
And I posed the question by
saying that you could not

01:33:49.140 --> 01:33:51.140
have been under any social
or political pressure

01:33:51.140 --> 01:33:53.150
to write a letter.

01:33:53.150 --> 01:33:54.900
And Tom, Jr. told me
that he was motivated

01:33:54.900 --> 01:33:56.430
to do so because
he was negotiating

01:33:56.430 --> 01:33:59.190
with the governors of
Kentucky and North Carolina

01:33:59.190 --> 01:34:03.030
to build the IBM plants
in Lexington and Raleigh,

01:34:03.030 --> 01:34:07.950
two major sites
potentially for IBM.

01:34:07.950 --> 01:34:11.522
But he told both governors
that he would not accept

01:34:11.522 --> 01:34:12.605
separate but equal at IBM.

01:34:15.800 --> 01:34:18.480
And both governors
said, bring the payroll

01:34:18.480 --> 01:34:21.120
and manage the people
any way you want to.

01:34:21.120 --> 01:34:24.510
Now, that's an early example
of not show me the money,

01:34:24.510 --> 01:34:26.730
but bring me the money.

01:34:26.730 --> 01:34:29.660
Tax and payroll dollars.

01:34:29.660 --> 01:34:33.270
Once again, I don't
care who you hate.

01:34:33.270 --> 01:34:36.540
You don't hate them more
than you love money.

01:34:36.540 --> 01:34:41.300
And on May 17, 1954, the Supreme
Court decision, the Brown

01:34:41.300 --> 01:34:44.450
decision, declaring
that separate but equal

01:34:44.450 --> 01:34:47.180
was inconsistent with the
principles of our nation.

01:34:47.180 --> 01:34:50.590
And in 1957, we got
the Civil Rights Act

01:34:50.590 --> 01:34:54.040
of 1957, Congress's first
civil rights legislation

01:34:54.040 --> 01:34:57.390
since reconstruction,
which established

01:34:57.390 --> 01:35:00.180
the US Department of Justice
as the guarantor of the right

01:35:00.180 --> 01:35:02.272
to vote.

01:35:02.272 --> 01:35:03.730
The act was a
presidential response

01:35:03.730 --> 01:35:07.215
to the political divisions that
followed the Brown decision.

01:35:07.215 --> 01:35:08.590
Now I want another
sidebar story.

01:35:08.590 --> 01:35:10.257
I want to talk about
the Brown decision.

01:35:12.530 --> 01:35:17.257
There were some players
in this decision--

01:35:19.880 --> 01:35:23.060
presidential candidate Dwight
Eisenhower, California Attorney

01:35:23.060 --> 01:35:27.170
General, later to be governor,
Earl Warren, the Brown case

01:35:27.170 --> 01:35:31.490
itself, the Supreme Court
Chief Justice Fred Vinson.

01:35:31.490 --> 01:35:37.570
And some of you know Professor
Charles Ogletree at Harvard,

01:35:37.570 --> 01:35:40.500
and he describes this story
very poignantly in his book,

01:35:40.500 --> 01:35:43.180
All Deliberate Speed.

01:35:43.180 --> 01:35:45.400
Dwight Eisenhower was a
candidate for President

01:35:45.400 --> 01:35:48.410
of the United States.

01:35:48.410 --> 01:35:52.220
Earl Warren was the
governor of California.

01:35:52.220 --> 01:35:53.840
Dwight Eisenhower
concluded that he

01:35:53.840 --> 01:35:55.940
needed the delegation
from California

01:35:55.940 --> 01:35:57.710
to win the nomination.

01:35:57.710 --> 01:36:01.280
And he told then Governor
Warren that if you will deliver

01:36:01.280 --> 01:36:05.260
the votes of
California for me, I

01:36:05.260 --> 01:36:08.710
will give you the first
appointment to the Supreme

01:36:08.710 --> 01:36:10.690
Court of the United States.

01:36:10.690 --> 01:36:13.390
The Brown case was moving
through the court system.

01:36:16.020 --> 01:36:18.720
Conservatives in
America were not

01:36:18.720 --> 01:36:20.880
worried about the Brown case.

01:36:20.880 --> 01:36:23.730
They knew where it was
headed, to the Supreme Court,

01:36:23.730 --> 01:36:25.470
but their people were in charge.

01:36:25.470 --> 01:36:27.978
Fred Vinson was
the Chief Justice.

01:36:27.978 --> 01:36:28.686
Fred Vinson died.

01:36:33.170 --> 01:36:38.900
As you have watched the process
of filling Supreme Court seats,

01:36:38.900 --> 01:36:44.390
those who worry about such
things had their short lists.

01:36:44.390 --> 01:36:46.820
They also had a problem.

01:36:46.820 --> 01:36:52.120
Dwight David Eisenhower had
made a deal with Earl Warren,

01:36:52.120 --> 01:36:54.860
and he chose to honor the deal.

01:36:54.860 --> 01:37:00.020
What he did in appointing Earl
Warren governor of California

01:37:00.020 --> 01:37:02.780
was he appointed Earl Warren
governor of California,

01:37:02.780 --> 01:37:05.390
previously attorney
general for California who

01:37:05.390 --> 01:37:08.720
orchestrated the internment of
the Japanese and had concluded

01:37:08.720 --> 01:37:11.790
that was a bad decision.

01:37:11.790 --> 01:37:14.850
And he led the Supreme
Court to the Brown decision

01:37:14.850 --> 01:37:25.400
that we now know
happened on May 17, 1954.

01:37:25.400 --> 01:37:29.530
The results of the various
actions that I've described

01:37:29.530 --> 01:37:32.290
would lay the foundation of the
social corporate and political

01:37:32.290 --> 01:37:34.690
behavior in the '60s and beyond.

01:37:34.690 --> 01:37:37.960
In 1962, President John
Kennedy initiated Plans

01:37:37.960 --> 01:37:40.660
for Progress, a program
providing US companies

01:37:40.660 --> 01:37:43.630
with the opportunity to
sign the document indicating

01:37:43.630 --> 01:37:46.790
their intent to hire
minority citizens.

01:37:46.790 --> 01:37:48.350
Active affirmative
action programs

01:37:48.350 --> 01:37:52.040
were launched with college
recruitment directed

01:37:52.040 --> 01:37:55.100
at black colleges.

01:37:55.100 --> 01:37:59.480
My hiring by IBM in 1967 was a
result of such an initiative.

01:37:59.480 --> 01:38:01.880
Another sidebar
story-- yesterday,

01:38:01.880 --> 01:38:04.180
I'm standing at LaGuardia.

01:38:04.180 --> 01:38:07.510
Yesterday was pure
hell at LaGuardia,

01:38:07.510 --> 01:38:10.390
the result of the day before.

01:38:10.390 --> 01:38:14.350
I find myself standing next
to a fellow, and I said,

01:38:14.350 --> 01:38:15.920
are you who I think you are?

01:38:15.920 --> 01:38:17.920
He just looks at me, and
he smiles, and he said,

01:38:17.920 --> 01:38:18.670
I think so.

01:38:21.560 --> 01:38:27.980
Bobby Kennedy, Jr. I said,
young fella, where do you live?

01:38:27.980 --> 01:38:30.498
Don't we live near each other?

01:38:30.498 --> 01:38:31.790
He said, I live in Mount Kisco.

01:38:31.790 --> 01:38:32.998
I said I live in Pound Ridge.

01:38:32.998 --> 01:38:34.930
The towns are right
next to each other.

01:38:34.930 --> 01:38:40.000
I said, look, I'm a
native of Springfield.

01:38:40.000 --> 01:38:42.400
1960, I was a freshman
in high school.

01:38:42.400 --> 01:38:44.690
My United States senator
was running for president.

01:38:44.690 --> 01:38:47.020
He came to town to
let us out of school.

01:38:47.020 --> 01:38:50.380
When I watched the interview
on 60 Minutes on Sunday,

01:38:50.380 --> 01:38:54.400
I told my friends that
man reminds me of Jack.

01:38:54.400 --> 01:38:57.950
The interview was with Barack.

01:38:57.950 --> 01:39:01.758
And he and I had a few
moments about his uncle.

01:39:04.630 --> 01:39:08.040
Companion to the
hiring initiatives,

01:39:08.040 --> 01:39:11.610
corporations launched
minority supplier programs

01:39:11.610 --> 01:39:13.610
that were equally important
because they pledged

01:39:13.610 --> 01:39:18.680
their support to
buy from companies

01:39:18.680 --> 01:39:20.420
owned by women or minorities.

01:39:20.420 --> 01:39:22.790
So the bigotry and
intolerance of the '40s

01:39:22.790 --> 01:39:24.710
sparked attitudinal
change in the '50s,

01:39:24.710 --> 01:39:26.580
and that led to
social, political,

01:39:26.580 --> 01:39:28.970
and corporate
activism in the '60s.

01:39:28.970 --> 01:39:31.560
A lot of water has passed
over the dam since then.

01:39:31.560 --> 01:39:34.667
50 years since the Brown
decision, where are we?

01:39:34.667 --> 01:39:36.500
Well, we can claim and
demonstrate progress,

01:39:36.500 --> 01:39:38.900
but we cannot hide the fact
that we are in a crisis,

01:39:38.900 --> 01:39:40.625
what I call a seminal moment.

01:39:40.625 --> 01:39:42.500
For me, a seminal moment
is when we encounter

01:39:42.500 --> 01:39:46.310
a crisis, a crisis that requires
the social and political will

01:39:46.310 --> 01:39:49.730
to use all of our resources
to combat a real or perceived

01:39:49.730 --> 01:39:50.720
threat.

01:39:50.720 --> 01:39:53.180
Pearl Harbor was
a seminal moment.

01:39:53.180 --> 01:39:56.330
Sputnik was a seminal moment.

01:39:56.330 --> 01:39:59.720
The gasoline crisis of the
'70s was a seminal moment,

01:39:59.720 --> 01:40:02.480
for the moment.

01:40:02.480 --> 01:40:03.013
We suffered.

01:40:03.013 --> 01:40:03.680
We sucked it up.

01:40:03.680 --> 01:40:06.058
We sat in lines
at gasoline pumps.

01:40:06.058 --> 01:40:07.850
Some of us got up at
4:00 AM in the morning

01:40:07.850 --> 01:40:10.220
to drive to gas
stations to get in line.

01:40:10.220 --> 01:40:12.160
We sacrificed, we walked.

01:40:12.160 --> 01:40:13.760
We took buses, we carpooled.

01:40:13.760 --> 01:40:15.560
We had discussions
at the highest levels

01:40:15.560 --> 01:40:18.213
of our government about the
need to wean ourselves off

01:40:18.213 --> 01:40:19.130
of our oil dependence.

01:40:19.130 --> 01:40:20.573
We stopped buying big cars.

01:40:20.573 --> 01:40:22.490
And for a moment in time,
we had a love affair

01:40:22.490 --> 01:40:26.390
with small cars,
gas conscious cars.

01:40:26.390 --> 01:40:30.620
Then the gas started flowing,
and the crisis ended.

01:40:30.620 --> 01:40:32.900
I think 9/11 was
a seminal moment.

01:40:32.900 --> 01:40:35.720
At least, it made us angry.

01:40:35.720 --> 01:40:38.120
Our seminal moment
today is, I believe,

01:40:38.120 --> 01:40:41.000
the need to develop and execute
a competitive national talent

01:40:41.000 --> 01:40:42.700
strategy.

01:40:42.700 --> 01:40:45.640
Our schools are not producing
students who can read, write,

01:40:45.640 --> 01:40:47.880
count, and think.

01:40:47.880 --> 01:40:49.810
A key founding principle
of the democracy

01:40:49.810 --> 01:40:52.010
was that a public
school education

01:40:52.010 --> 01:40:54.470
would provide an
educated citizenry that

01:40:54.470 --> 01:40:59.010
could understand and debate
issues, draw conclusions, go

01:40:59.010 --> 01:41:04.740
into a voting booth,
and elect our leaders.

01:41:04.740 --> 01:41:07.220
Not only are we not
educated, we don't vote.

01:41:10.100 --> 01:41:18.060
If you go to
tomorrowsworkforce.org,

01:41:18.060 --> 01:41:21.060
this website describes where
American high school students

01:41:21.060 --> 01:41:25.030
rank versus their peers
in other countries.

01:41:25.030 --> 01:41:28.690
And it ain't pretty.

01:41:28.690 --> 01:41:30.530
Of the 21 countries
participating

01:41:30.530 --> 01:41:33.740
in the third International
Mathematics and Science Study,

01:41:33.740 --> 01:41:37.130
American high school seniors
outperformed only students

01:41:37.130 --> 01:41:41.520
from Cyprus and South Africa.

01:41:41.520 --> 01:41:46.180
It ranked behind students in
Sweden, Canada, New Zealand,

01:41:46.180 --> 01:41:49.530
Russia, and the Czech Republic.

01:41:49.530 --> 01:41:50.990
A recent study by
the Organization

01:41:50.990 --> 01:41:54.752
for Economic Cooperation
and Development

01:41:54.752 --> 01:41:56.210
showed that America's
literacy rate

01:41:56.210 --> 01:42:00.410
is average among the nations
of the industrialized world

01:42:00.410 --> 01:42:03.850
and that our high school
graduation rate, 73%,

01:42:03.850 --> 01:42:07.200
is one of the lowest in
the industrial nations.

01:42:07.200 --> 01:42:09.930
We follow Denmark, Norway,
Germany, Japan, Poland,

01:42:09.930 --> 01:42:17.650
Switzerland, Finland, Greece,
Japan, Australia, Canada,

01:42:17.650 --> 01:42:20.870
Belgium, Switzerland, the
Netherlands, France, Germany,

01:42:20.870 --> 01:42:22.840
Sweden, and Ireland.

01:42:22.840 --> 01:42:24.424
It's rather intimidating.

01:42:30.150 --> 01:42:31.890
That is why this is
a seminal moment,

01:42:31.890 --> 01:42:34.230
because the failure of
our educational system

01:42:34.230 --> 01:42:36.270
is undermining our
competitiveness

01:42:36.270 --> 01:42:37.650
in a global marketplace.

01:42:37.650 --> 01:42:40.413
I travel all over the
world, and I'm amazed

01:42:40.413 --> 01:42:41.580
that I can talk to children.

01:42:41.580 --> 01:42:44.640
I speak one language,
and I meet kids

01:42:44.640 --> 01:42:46.730
who speak their
language and English.

01:42:46.730 --> 01:42:49.915
They tell a joke
in other countries.

01:42:49.915 --> 01:42:51.790
You speak three languages,
you're trilingual.

01:42:51.790 --> 01:42:53.750
If you speak two languages,
you're bilingual.

01:42:53.750 --> 01:42:56.600
If you speak one
language, you're American.

01:43:00.030 --> 01:43:02.350
A recent issue about
the national anthem

01:43:02.350 --> 01:43:05.780
being sung in Spanish,
president of the United States

01:43:05.780 --> 01:43:08.660
and the Vice President commented
that the US national anthem

01:43:08.660 --> 01:43:13.300
should only be sung in English.

01:43:13.300 --> 01:43:17.140
And I was kind of
overwhelmed by that.

01:43:17.140 --> 01:43:20.770
I can't imagine anyone
singing my national anthem

01:43:20.770 --> 01:43:25.697
in their language and flying
planes into our buildings.

01:43:25.697 --> 01:43:28.155
I'd like to see a lot of people
singing the national anthem

01:43:28.155 --> 01:43:29.085
in their countries.

01:43:33.677 --> 01:43:35.510
We're having a national
debate about English

01:43:35.510 --> 01:43:36.710
being the national language.

01:43:36.710 --> 01:43:39.593
I think that's a flawed debate.

01:43:39.593 --> 01:43:41.760
I think English is going
to be the dominant language

01:43:41.760 --> 01:43:42.882
in the United States.

01:43:42.882 --> 01:43:44.340
And if we conclude
there is a risk,

01:43:44.340 --> 01:43:46.845
we can attack that where
the problem emerges.

01:43:46.845 --> 01:43:49.710
But I think we need to
have a national dialogue

01:43:49.710 --> 01:43:51.480
about requiring American
children to speak

01:43:51.480 --> 01:43:55.080
more than one language and
to do so fluently before they

01:43:55.080 --> 01:43:57.450
can graduate from high school.

01:43:57.450 --> 01:43:59.640
The Chinese government
has made a commitment

01:43:59.640 --> 01:44:03.200
to Chinese children to
learn to speak English.

01:44:03.200 --> 01:44:05.630
That is a strong message.

01:44:05.630 --> 01:44:07.270
I was in a meeting
one day recently,

01:44:07.270 --> 01:44:09.660
just a group of us talking
about world affairs.

01:44:09.660 --> 01:44:11.427
And I posed a thought.

01:44:11.427 --> 01:44:13.760
I said, I wonder if the Chinese
government does not want

01:44:13.760 --> 01:44:15.680
Bin Laden as much as we do.

01:44:15.680 --> 01:44:17.930
I wonder if they got him,
if the conversation wouldn't

01:44:17.930 --> 01:44:19.280
go like this.

01:44:19.280 --> 01:44:23.400
Chinese leader--
Mr. Bin Laden, you

01:44:23.400 --> 01:44:25.150
are destroying
private property when

01:44:25.150 --> 01:44:28.030
you fly those planes into
buildings in the United States,

01:44:28.030 --> 01:44:30.250
especially New York.

01:44:30.250 --> 01:44:32.622
Bin Laden-- what do you
mean private property?

01:44:32.622 --> 01:44:33.580
They are the Americans.

01:44:33.580 --> 01:44:35.230
They are devils.

01:44:35.230 --> 01:44:40.960
Chinese leader-- Mr. Bin Laden,
you must have missed a meeting.

01:44:40.960 --> 01:44:43.540
You are not with the strategy.

01:44:43.540 --> 01:44:47.120
The Americans, they
are not our enemies.

01:44:47.120 --> 01:44:49.670
They are our business partners.

01:44:49.670 --> 01:44:52.440
We are investing
in their country.

01:44:52.440 --> 01:44:55.510
We may even own those
buildings one day.

01:44:55.510 --> 01:44:57.600
And we are not in a hurry.

01:44:57.600 --> 01:44:59.060
One day will come.

01:44:59.060 --> 01:45:03.710
Mr. bin Laden, no more planes.

01:45:03.710 --> 01:45:07.190
You are destroying our
long-term investment.

01:45:07.190 --> 01:45:09.280
Hold the thought.

01:45:09.280 --> 01:45:12.340
When we look back over history,
the most advanced civilizations

01:45:12.340 --> 01:45:15.200
were the ones that had the
highest degree of technology

01:45:15.200 --> 01:45:18.880
however technology was
defined in their time.

01:45:18.880 --> 01:45:21.250
We have been viewed as the
most advanced civilization

01:45:21.250 --> 01:45:22.450
of our time.

01:45:22.450 --> 01:45:24.490
But change is coming.

01:45:24.490 --> 01:45:27.100
A key defining example of
the technological prowess

01:45:27.100 --> 01:45:28.990
of your civilization
is the number

01:45:28.990 --> 01:45:32.100
of engineers you produce.

01:45:32.100 --> 01:45:35.450
I have not forgotten
where I am--

01:45:35.450 --> 01:45:37.370
America's premier
institution of higher

01:45:37.370 --> 01:45:40.380
learning in the field
of math and science.

01:45:40.380 --> 01:45:43.620
Well, America produces
75,000 engineers a year,

01:45:43.620 --> 01:45:49.550
but Russia produces 85,000,
Japan, 105,000, India, 130,000,

01:45:49.550 --> 01:45:53.120
and China, well over 200,000.

01:45:53.120 --> 01:45:55.530
Well, if they can
produce more engineers

01:45:55.530 --> 01:45:58.280
and they can speak their
language and English, who

01:45:58.280 --> 01:46:01.190
is preparing to
compete for being

01:46:01.190 --> 01:46:03.380
the most advanced civilization?

01:46:03.380 --> 01:46:06.320
Whose children will be
the most competitive

01:46:06.320 --> 01:46:09.060
in a global marketplace?

01:46:09.060 --> 01:46:11.650
Not ours.

01:46:11.650 --> 01:46:14.980
If you look at the
Fortune 25 in 1955,

01:46:14.980 --> 01:46:19.060
you see names like US Steel,
Bethlehem Steel, Gulf Oil,

01:46:19.060 --> 01:46:22.960
Swift, International
Harvester, Union Carbide, RCA,

01:46:22.960 --> 01:46:25.930
Firestone Tire and
Rubber, companies

01:46:25.930 --> 01:46:29.140
that were there in 1955 and
either don't exist today,

01:46:29.140 --> 01:46:33.920
have been bought, or are
just far down the list.

01:46:33.920 --> 01:46:39.330
Just like they had no long-term
guarantee of remaining on top,

01:46:39.330 --> 01:46:43.040
America has no such
guarantee either.

01:46:43.040 --> 01:46:46.300
We are concerned
about job flight.

01:46:46.300 --> 01:46:48.960
Those are not our jobs.

01:46:48.960 --> 01:46:51.030
They are not American jobs.

01:46:51.030 --> 01:46:54.350
We do not have an
entitlement to them.

01:46:54.350 --> 01:46:57.440
How many people in this room
own stock in a global company?

01:47:01.000 --> 01:47:02.650
Well, we're conflicted.

01:47:02.650 --> 01:47:05.610
We want jobs here and
we want to make money.

01:47:05.610 --> 01:47:08.990
Indeed, we expect and
demand to make money.

01:47:08.990 --> 01:47:10.160
We can't have it both ways.

01:47:10.160 --> 01:47:12.160
Those countries are making
more of an investment

01:47:12.160 --> 01:47:14.620
in their schools than we are.

01:47:14.620 --> 01:47:17.590
40 or 50 years ago, when an
IBM was looking for a community

01:47:17.590 --> 01:47:21.720
to build a plant, we had
a handful of criteria.

01:47:21.720 --> 01:47:24.120
No labor conflict,
quality transportation,

01:47:24.120 --> 01:47:27.150
quality housing, good schools,
and a good university system.

01:47:27.150 --> 01:47:30.170
You can find that
today in many places.

01:47:30.170 --> 01:47:34.120
Last week, in a New York
Times and Wall Street Journal

01:47:34.120 --> 01:47:42.510
article, Mr. Bernanke,
at the Federal Reserve,

01:47:42.510 --> 01:47:45.540
talked about disparities
in education and training

01:47:45.540 --> 01:47:49.436
as the single greatest source
of the long-term increase

01:47:49.436 --> 01:47:50.864
in inequality.

01:47:54.200 --> 01:47:56.450
If the head of one of
our global companies

01:47:56.450 --> 01:47:58.550
is visiting a country
today and meeting

01:47:58.550 --> 01:48:00.560
with the president
of that country,

01:48:00.560 --> 01:48:02.390
the business leader
is likely to be asked,

01:48:02.390 --> 01:48:04.620
what are you doing
for my country?

01:48:04.620 --> 01:48:06.810
In the past, the
company's CEO would

01:48:06.810 --> 01:48:10.230
have said, well, I'm paying this
much in taxes in your country.

01:48:10.230 --> 01:48:13.200
I'm making this much in
charitable contributions.

01:48:13.200 --> 01:48:15.300
Today, that answer
will not be acceptable.

01:48:15.300 --> 01:48:17.340
The country
president will say, I

01:48:17.340 --> 01:48:20.590
know how much money
your company is making.

01:48:20.590 --> 01:48:23.245
And I know how much
you made in my country.

01:48:23.245 --> 01:48:24.620
The only answer
I'm interested in

01:48:24.620 --> 01:48:29.980
is how many jobs you are
putting in my country.

01:48:29.980 --> 01:48:32.030
The country president
would be on point

01:48:32.030 --> 01:48:36.610
in focusing on the source of the
conflict with the shareholder.

01:48:36.610 --> 01:48:38.650
Global companies
require a network

01:48:38.650 --> 01:48:41.900
of successful local economies.

01:48:41.900 --> 01:48:46.250
A global company cannot be
successful by being successful

01:48:46.250 --> 01:48:48.440
only in the United States.

01:48:48.440 --> 01:48:51.270
You can have a great
year here and a bad year

01:48:51.270 --> 01:48:53.660
in enough other places to
have a bad business year.

01:48:56.260 --> 01:49:00.290
Global financial success
means multiple success.

01:49:00.290 --> 01:49:03.850
Tom Watson, Sr.
joined IBM in 1914.

01:49:03.850 --> 01:49:05.290
It was CTR Company at the time.

01:49:05.290 --> 01:49:07.600
He became the president in 1915.

01:49:07.600 --> 01:49:10.870
In 1923, he established
a subsidiary, World Trade

01:49:10.870 --> 01:49:15.170
Corporation, then changed
the name to IBM in 1924.

01:49:15.170 --> 01:49:19.140
He had a vision that it
would be a global business.

01:49:19.140 --> 01:49:22.080
IBM made its first billion
dollars outside of the US

01:49:22.080 --> 01:49:24.730
in 1965.

01:49:24.730 --> 01:49:27.760
1975 was the first
year more than half

01:49:27.760 --> 01:49:30.840
the company's revenue came from
outside of the United States--

01:49:30.840 --> 01:49:33.058
1975.

01:49:33.058 --> 01:49:35.350
I point that out to you
because three major competitors

01:49:35.350 --> 01:49:36.160
of IBM--

01:49:36.160 --> 01:49:37.810
Apple, Microsoft, and Dell--

01:49:37.810 --> 01:49:42.230
didn't exist in 1975.

01:49:42.230 --> 01:49:45.980
And then in 1993, that was the
first year that more than half

01:49:45.980 --> 01:49:49.700
of IBM's people were outside
of the United States.

01:49:49.700 --> 01:49:51.950
Now let me come back to the
Chinese leader and the Bin

01:49:51.950 --> 01:49:53.290
Laden discussion.

01:49:53.290 --> 01:49:55.790
If those nations where we see
the greatest economic growth--

01:49:55.790 --> 01:49:59.420
China, India, Brazil,
Russia, former Eastern Bloc--

01:49:59.420 --> 01:50:01.880
are competing with
us for opportunity,

01:50:01.880 --> 01:50:04.850
well, if they're making
a more focused investment

01:50:04.850 --> 01:50:07.250
in their country,
particularly education,

01:50:07.250 --> 01:50:12.080
and investing in the United
States, what picture do we see?

01:50:12.080 --> 01:50:14.120
When I said hold
the thought, I meant

01:50:14.120 --> 01:50:16.490
do we see a seminal moment?

01:50:16.490 --> 01:50:18.080
I don't mean Pearl
Harbor or 9/11.

01:50:18.080 --> 01:50:22.790
I don't mean Sputnik, where
we will have airway drills

01:50:22.790 --> 01:50:25.130
and schoolchildren
that will practice what

01:50:25.130 --> 01:50:28.180
to do if there's an attack.

01:50:28.180 --> 01:50:31.810
The essence of my imaginary
Chinese leader Bin Laden

01:50:31.810 --> 01:50:37.730
discussion is that someone
wins, but no bullets are fired.

01:50:37.730 --> 01:50:40.000
I did not mean to offend
by using the Chinese leader

01:50:40.000 --> 01:50:40.750
in the discussion.

01:50:40.750 --> 01:50:44.220
He was just an effective
tool for my debate.

01:50:44.220 --> 01:50:46.920
I said earlier that I'm stressed
over the absence of student

01:50:46.920 --> 01:50:48.180
activism.

01:50:48.180 --> 01:50:50.850
I'm concerned about
what's on their minds.

01:50:50.850 --> 01:50:57.220
Here are four recent incidents.

01:50:57.220 --> 01:51:02.120
Here in Massachusetts, last
fall at Babson, some students

01:51:02.120 --> 01:51:07.503
dressed up as Celtics
and in blackface.

01:51:07.503 --> 01:51:09.670
Two weeks ago at Guilford
College in North Carolina,

01:51:09.670 --> 01:51:15.950
I was there to speak at
Bennett Newspaper Story.

01:51:15.950 --> 01:51:19.620
It has been reported that on
the morning of January 20, three

01:51:19.620 --> 01:51:21.750
students, two from Guilford
College, one visiting

01:51:21.750 --> 01:51:25.200
from North Carolina State,
all from the West Bank,

01:51:25.200 --> 01:51:27.390
were attacked by Guilford
College football players

01:51:27.390 --> 01:51:30.227
in what is being
labeled a hate crime.

01:51:30.227 --> 01:51:32.810
This was a crime that occurred
on a Greensboro, North Carolina

01:51:32.810 --> 01:51:35.300
campus, a Quaker college.

01:51:35.300 --> 01:51:38.960
Greensboro, the
home of the lunch

01:51:38.960 --> 01:51:41.386
counter sit-ins that
changed the nation.

01:51:44.740 --> 01:51:46.980
Now, we ask the question--

01:51:46.980 --> 01:51:49.480
how did such an act of violence
occur in an environment that

01:51:49.480 --> 01:51:52.510
so adamantly opposes violence?

01:51:52.510 --> 01:51:56.600
We have two white students
and one black student

01:51:56.600 --> 01:51:58.220
allegedly beating
on these students

01:51:58.220 --> 01:52:02.520
and calling them sand
niggers and terrorists.

01:52:02.520 --> 01:52:06.380
So we go back to the argument
of why do they hate us?

01:52:06.380 --> 01:52:10.850
Texas, students at Tarleton
State University in Texas

01:52:10.850 --> 01:52:12.740
held a Martin
Luther King, Jr. Day

01:52:12.740 --> 01:52:15.950
party that ridiculed
black culture

01:52:15.950 --> 01:52:17.570
by having students
play upon the worst

01:52:17.570 --> 01:52:20.450
stereotypes, such as students
wearing gang apparel,

01:52:20.450 --> 01:52:22.910
dressing up as Aunt Jemima,
drinking malt liquor,

01:52:22.910 --> 01:52:24.920
and eating fried chicken.

01:52:24.920 --> 01:52:26.420
Education officials
at the school

01:52:26.420 --> 01:52:27.920
found out about the
party by looking

01:52:27.920 --> 01:52:29.690
at pictures of the
party that were

01:52:29.690 --> 01:52:35.330
displayed on the social
networking site Facebook.com.

01:52:35.330 --> 01:52:36.770
University president
Dennis McCabe

01:52:36.770 --> 01:52:38.915
said the photographs
were reprehensible.

01:52:46.350 --> 01:52:48.062
Princeton University.

01:52:50.954 --> 01:53:03.150
Princeton is a renowned
institution of higher learning.

01:53:05.930 --> 01:53:12.390
And recently, the student
newspaper at Princeton,

01:53:12.390 --> 01:53:14.670
[? Velvet ?] Chan, a senior
at Princeton University,

01:53:14.670 --> 01:53:18.210
was stunned when she encountered
an article in broken English

01:53:18.210 --> 01:53:21.630
in the annual joke issue
of The Student Daily

01:53:21.630 --> 01:53:24.390
parodying an
Asian-American student who

01:53:24.390 --> 01:53:26.923
had filed a civil rights
complaint against Student.

01:53:26.923 --> 01:53:28.590
The editor in chief
said their attention

01:53:28.590 --> 01:53:32.090
was to spark dialogue on race.

01:53:32.090 --> 01:53:33.920
The article read,
"Hi, Princeton.

01:53:33.920 --> 01:53:35.470
Remember me?

01:53:35.470 --> 01:53:37.130
The parody began.

01:53:37.130 --> 01:53:41.270
"I so good at math and science,
perfect 2,400 SAT score.

01:53:41.270 --> 01:53:43.090
Ring bells?

01:53:43.090 --> 01:53:46.960
Just in cases, let me
refresh your memories.

01:53:46.960 --> 01:53:48.790
I the super smart Asian.

01:53:48.790 --> 01:53:53.300
Princeton, the super dumb
college, not accept me.

01:53:53.300 --> 01:53:57.290
What is wrong with
you no colored people?

01:53:57.290 --> 01:53:59.270
Yellow people make
the world go round.

01:53:59.270 --> 01:54:02.030
We cook greasy food,
wash your clothes,

01:54:02.030 --> 01:54:03.518
let you copy our homework."

01:54:08.410 --> 01:54:10.540
The Princetonian's
editor in chief,

01:54:10.540 --> 01:54:12.790
who is of Indian descent,
said the staff was trying

01:54:12.790 --> 01:54:15.130
to put the article behind them.

01:54:15.130 --> 01:54:18.410
"We embraced racist language
in order to strangle it,"

01:54:18.410 --> 01:54:19.302
she said.

01:54:19.302 --> 01:54:21.010
"At its worst, the
column was a bad joke.

01:54:21.010 --> 01:54:23.020
At its best, it provokes
serious thought."

01:54:23.020 --> 01:54:24.437
Well, she's right
about that part.

01:54:27.670 --> 01:54:31.430
Where is our student
attention to global warming,

01:54:31.430 --> 01:54:32.900
the war in Iraq?

01:54:32.900 --> 01:54:34.390
I figured that one
out, by the way.

01:54:34.390 --> 01:54:36.098
My generation was
concerned about Vietnam

01:54:36.098 --> 01:54:37.767
because of the draft.

01:54:37.767 --> 01:54:39.850
Where are the students'
concern about immigration,

01:54:39.850 --> 01:54:42.490
the quality of our air and
water, continuing civil rights

01:54:42.490 --> 01:54:44.650
debates, stem cell
research, the lack of health

01:54:44.650 --> 01:54:47.140
care for all Americans, the
increasing competitive job

01:54:47.140 --> 01:54:48.640
advantage of other nations?

01:54:48.640 --> 01:54:52.730
And I believe most importantly,
the failure of our schools.

01:54:52.730 --> 01:54:58.890
By the way, I don't mean
to criticize them for not

01:54:58.890 --> 01:55:00.317
aggressively taking on the war.

01:55:00.317 --> 01:55:02.400
I do think that the fact
that our generation dealt

01:55:02.400 --> 01:55:05.912
with the draft was important.

01:55:05.912 --> 01:55:08.370
Now unless you think I'm just
concerned about our students,

01:55:08.370 --> 01:55:11.540
I'm just as concerned
about business leaders.

01:55:11.540 --> 01:55:15.052
I entered high school with
some very strong views.

01:55:15.052 --> 01:55:16.760
I thought that because
of Jackie Robinson

01:55:16.760 --> 01:55:19.383
integrating baseball, Harry
Truman's executive order

01:55:19.383 --> 01:55:21.800
integrating the military, and
the Brown decision declaring

01:55:21.800 --> 01:55:23.928
separate but equal
unacceptable, that a generation

01:55:23.928 --> 01:55:25.970
of boys that went to school
together, played ball

01:55:25.970 --> 01:55:29.390
together, and died together
would change America.

01:55:29.390 --> 01:55:31.710
I was wrong.

01:55:31.710 --> 01:55:33.800
But now the debates
are far bigger,

01:55:33.800 --> 01:55:38.360
and for very real reasons,
bigger than black and white.

01:55:38.360 --> 01:55:40.702
Those conflicts still exist.

01:55:40.702 --> 01:55:42.410
But the inclusion of
our Hispanic, Asian,

01:55:42.410 --> 01:55:46.770
and Native American, disabled,
the gay lesbian debate,

01:55:46.770 --> 01:55:52.840
career equity for women,
all of that is important.

01:55:52.840 --> 01:55:54.390
And the issue of
age may turn out

01:55:54.390 --> 01:55:56.850
to be the premiere
business-related issue

01:55:56.850 --> 01:55:59.010
of our generation.

01:55:59.010 --> 01:56:02.750
Regarding race, we have to
have a meaningful discussion

01:56:02.750 --> 01:56:04.810
about affirmative action.

01:56:04.810 --> 01:56:08.410
I believe we have to adopt
the concept of mend it,

01:56:08.410 --> 01:56:09.160
don't end it.

01:56:09.160 --> 01:56:11.870
But my version of mend it,
don't end it is very simple.

01:56:11.870 --> 01:56:13.840
We have to end the focus
on affirmative action

01:56:13.840 --> 01:56:16.750
as a race and gender dialogue.

01:56:16.750 --> 01:56:19.040
Affirmative action has
to be about disadvantage,

01:56:19.040 --> 01:56:20.593
and people who
are white and poor

01:56:20.593 --> 01:56:22.510
have to be able to benefit
from our commitment

01:56:22.510 --> 01:56:23.920
to affirmative action.

01:56:23.920 --> 01:56:27.160
Because we need everybody
on the playing field.

01:56:27.160 --> 01:56:29.560
For me to say to
someone white, somewhere

01:56:29.560 --> 01:56:31.840
in your history,
somebody owned slaves,

01:56:31.840 --> 01:56:34.250
it doesn't matter anymore.

01:56:34.250 --> 01:56:35.750
We have sportswriters
who would like

01:56:35.750 --> 01:56:38.320
to tell us that America's
team is located in Dallas

01:56:38.320 --> 01:56:41.000
or that American
sport is baseball.

01:56:41.000 --> 01:56:47.800
Well, America's team
is its workforce.

01:56:47.800 --> 01:56:50.890
In America, sport is innovation.

01:56:50.890 --> 01:56:53.110
Those workers and
that innovation

01:56:53.110 --> 01:56:55.610
are in a global marketplace.

01:56:55.610 --> 01:56:59.080
And that is what we bring
to the economic table--

01:56:59.080 --> 01:57:01.445
our workforce.

01:57:01.445 --> 01:57:03.820
We have to help all of our
people be prepared to compete.

01:57:03.820 --> 01:57:06.370
We can't win in our
local neighborhoods

01:57:06.370 --> 01:57:08.110
or individual
constituency groups.

01:57:08.110 --> 01:57:10.690
We can only win as Americans.

01:57:10.690 --> 01:57:13.210
So back to my seminal
moment, the college students

01:57:13.210 --> 01:57:15.460
need to see clearly the
responsibilities, the dangers,

01:57:15.460 --> 01:57:17.615
and the passions of their time.

01:57:17.615 --> 01:57:18.990
They need to see
them and agitate

01:57:18.990 --> 01:57:20.772
for healthy spirited debate.

01:57:20.772 --> 01:57:22.230
I think what these
two young ladies

01:57:22.230 --> 01:57:25.973
did this morning is
consistent with that.

01:57:25.973 --> 01:57:27.890
They need to galvanize
around real issues that

01:57:27.890 --> 01:57:29.840
are important to
their generation,

01:57:29.840 --> 01:57:33.770
as racial segregation and the
war in Vietnam were to ours.

01:57:33.770 --> 01:57:38.470
They need to spark debate
and grab us by the neck.

01:57:38.470 --> 01:57:40.220
A couple of years ago,
we had a major case

01:57:40.220 --> 01:57:43.290
come before the US Supreme
Court, the Michigan case.

01:57:43.290 --> 01:57:45.200
It generated more print
of the court briefs

01:57:45.200 --> 01:57:47.510
than any Supreme
Court case in history.

01:57:47.510 --> 01:57:49.460
IBM participated in
that case, and I was

01:57:49.460 --> 01:57:51.740
proud to help write the brief.

01:57:51.740 --> 01:57:57.110
IBM's involvement came about
because Dr. John Slaughter,

01:57:57.110 --> 01:58:00.780
then a member of the board,
former president of Occidental

01:58:00.780 --> 01:58:02.490
College, now the head
of National Action

01:58:02.490 --> 01:58:04.110
Council for Minorities
in Engineering,

01:58:04.110 --> 01:58:06.920
and Chuck Vest, a
member of the IBM board,

01:58:06.920 --> 01:58:12.240
then the president of MIT,
both pushed IBM, and Dupont,

01:58:12.240 --> 01:58:15.420
and Stanford, and MIT, and the
National Academy of Engineering

01:58:15.420 --> 01:58:17.980
to focus on it.

01:58:17.980 --> 01:58:19.505
And what we did was
say, look, we're

01:58:19.505 --> 01:58:22.130
not going to take a position on
the mathematical process that's

01:58:22.130 --> 01:58:23.620
being used by the university.

01:58:23.620 --> 01:58:26.310
But we do want to make
a couple of points.

01:58:26.310 --> 01:58:30.080
First, we argued that if the
universities of the United

01:58:30.080 --> 01:58:32.593
States don't produce a
diverse set of graduates,

01:58:32.593 --> 01:58:34.760
they're contributing to the
competitive disadvantage

01:58:34.760 --> 01:58:37.010
of the United States
business community.

01:58:37.010 --> 01:58:39.020
And second, we
wanted to redefine

01:58:39.020 --> 01:58:40.520
the concept of customer.

01:58:40.520 --> 01:58:44.920
We made the case that the
parents who pay the tuition--

01:58:44.920 --> 01:58:47.183
no disrespect to the parents
who are paying tuition--

01:58:47.183 --> 01:58:48.850
but that the parents
who pay the tuition

01:58:48.850 --> 01:58:51.072
are not the customers
at a college.

01:58:51.072 --> 01:58:53.280
The customers are
the employers who

01:58:53.280 --> 01:58:57.910
buy the finished product,
who produce the jobs.

01:58:57.910 --> 01:59:00.602
Education has always been our
strength, a differentiator

01:59:00.602 --> 01:59:01.875
in the global marketplace.

01:59:05.700 --> 01:59:08.430
We're only going to
win through education.

01:59:08.430 --> 01:59:11.550
We need national standards
and performance expectations.

01:59:11.550 --> 01:59:14.920
We cannot tolerate schools
in our largest cities

01:59:14.920 --> 01:59:18.380
or population centers
that are underfunded

01:59:18.380 --> 01:59:19.620
and underperforming.

01:59:19.620 --> 01:59:22.310
We cannot accept states
like Alabama and Mississippi

01:59:22.310 --> 01:59:25.550
consistently being at the bottom
of the educational performance

01:59:25.550 --> 01:59:26.240
letter.

01:59:26.240 --> 01:59:28.160
All of our children
are important

01:59:28.160 --> 01:59:29.330
in a global economic war.

01:59:29.330 --> 01:59:34.350
They are our troops, and
we need to develop them.

01:59:34.350 --> 01:59:36.210
So I lobby you for
a renewed commitment

01:59:36.210 --> 01:59:39.390
to defining and attacking the
responsibilities the dangerous

01:59:39.390 --> 01:59:41.370
and the passions of our time.

01:59:41.370 --> 01:59:43.740
The young have to be
appropriately distrustful,

01:59:43.740 --> 01:59:45.660
unhappy, and challenging
of the decisions

01:59:45.660 --> 01:59:48.720
and execution of those
decisions by their elders.

01:59:48.720 --> 01:59:51.210
Those elders have got to
see far more clearly than we

01:59:51.210 --> 01:59:53.343
are doing today.

01:59:53.343 --> 01:59:54.760
We have to make
decisions that are

01:59:54.760 --> 01:59:57.160
in the best interests
of our nation, not

01:59:57.160 --> 01:59:59.528
our company or our community.

01:59:59.528 --> 02:00:01.570
We have to think about
our country long-term, not

02:00:01.570 --> 02:00:04.630
three to five years.

02:00:04.630 --> 02:00:07.300
We have to see the demographic
changes that are underway

02:00:07.300 --> 02:00:09.070
in the United States.

02:00:09.070 --> 02:00:11.920
Our nation today has 90
million people of color.

02:00:11.920 --> 02:00:14.170
That is larger than
individual populations

02:00:14.170 --> 02:00:18.050
of Argentina, Australia, Canada,
France, England, Spain, Italy,

02:00:18.050 --> 02:00:21.710
Germany, South Korea,
South Africa, and Vietnam.

02:00:21.710 --> 02:00:25.650
Our Hispanic population,
the largest minority group.

02:00:25.650 --> 02:00:30.420
By 2050, America will be 50%
white, 25% Hispanic, and 25%

02:00:30.420 --> 02:00:32.010
everybody else.

02:00:32.010 --> 02:00:34.680
Our elected officials
in the marketplace

02:00:34.680 --> 02:00:38.063
reflect that change.

02:00:38.063 --> 02:00:39.730
It is important that
Nancy Pelosi is now

02:00:39.730 --> 02:00:41.050
the Speaker of the House.

02:00:41.050 --> 02:00:43.170
That's worth noting.

02:00:43.170 --> 02:00:45.460
But the news about
her elevation has

02:00:45.460 --> 02:00:47.170
overshadowed some
other elevations, which

02:00:47.170 --> 02:00:48.820
I think are very important.

02:00:48.820 --> 02:00:53.110
Charlie Rangel is now the
chairman of Ways and Means;

02:00:53.110 --> 02:00:56.090
John Conyers, chair of
the Judiciary Committee;

02:00:56.090 --> 02:00:58.090
Bennie Thompson, the chair
of Homeland Security;

02:00:58.090 --> 02:01:00.550
Juanita McDonald, the chair
of House Administration.

02:01:00.550 --> 02:01:02.210
They're all black.

02:01:02.210 --> 02:01:05.600
Silvestre Reyes, the chair of
Intelligence, Nydia Velázquez,

02:01:05.600 --> 02:01:07.820
the chair of Small
Business-- they're Hispanic.

02:01:10.410 --> 02:01:13.890
Louise Slaughter, a white
woman, chair of Rules,

02:01:13.890 --> 02:01:15.980
and the most conservative
industry in the world,

02:01:15.980 --> 02:01:19.660
the banking community,
now has to deal

02:01:19.660 --> 02:01:24.080
with a gay white man, graduate
of Harvard, from Boston.

02:01:24.080 --> 02:01:25.870
And I know that the
bank-- because I

02:01:25.870 --> 02:01:27.520
deal with these banking CEOs.

02:01:27.520 --> 02:01:29.284
I know that this is
their worst nightmare.

02:01:34.030 --> 02:01:36.700
The Asian, black, Hispanic,
and women's caucuses

02:01:36.700 --> 02:01:38.140
are gaining in strength.

02:01:38.140 --> 02:01:40.330
They've developed their
agendas, and they're

02:01:40.330 --> 02:01:42.205
focusing on connections.

02:01:42.205 --> 02:01:44.830
We have reached the point where
the interests of the minorities

02:01:44.830 --> 02:01:47.050
and women in the nation
are not separate from those

02:01:47.050 --> 02:01:48.070
of business.

02:01:48.070 --> 02:01:50.480
Our destinies are intertwined.

02:01:50.480 --> 02:01:53.500
I have met privately with the
CEOs of General Motors, Daimler

02:01:53.500 --> 02:01:55.480
Chrysler, Hallmark
Card, Sylvania, Pepsi,

02:01:55.480 --> 02:01:58.240
the Royal Bank of Canada,
JP Morgan Chase, Merck,

02:01:58.240 --> 02:02:01.030
Wyeth Lockheed Martin, American
Express, Campbell's Soup,

02:02:01.030 --> 02:02:04.090
Herman Miller, Sodexo, Eli
Lilly, the Southern Company,

02:02:04.090 --> 02:02:07.510
UBS, and a group
of CEOs in Tokyo.

02:02:07.510 --> 02:02:12.370
All of the discussions
were about their customers.

02:02:12.370 --> 02:02:14.140
And they increasingly
understand and want

02:02:14.140 --> 02:02:17.380
to execute within
the framework of what

02:02:17.380 --> 02:02:20.230
I tell them are two and the
only two meaningful goals

02:02:20.230 --> 02:02:21.770
of a diversity strategy.

02:02:21.770 --> 02:02:24.315
How do you identify, attract,
and retain the best people,

02:02:24.315 --> 02:02:25.690
and how do you
create a workplace

02:02:25.690 --> 02:02:30.050
where they can perform
to your maximum benefit?

02:02:30.050 --> 02:02:32.170
The investments
that we must make

02:02:32.170 --> 02:02:35.060
and the strategies
that we must develop

02:02:35.060 --> 02:02:37.573
will not yield overnight fixes.

02:02:37.573 --> 02:02:38.990
We need to understand
that excepts

02:02:38.990 --> 02:02:40.890
the absence of overnight fixes.

02:02:40.890 --> 02:02:42.217
But we must sit down together.

02:02:42.217 --> 02:02:44.300
Black, white, brown, red,
or yellow, young or old,

02:02:44.300 --> 02:02:47.750
gay or straight, male or female,
able-bodied or physically

02:02:47.750 --> 02:02:52.380
challenged, we are all in this
together when we win together

02:02:52.380 --> 02:02:56.520
or lose as a nation together.

02:02:56.520 --> 02:02:58.040
The responsibilities,
the dangers,

02:02:58.040 --> 02:03:01.900
and the passions of our
time belong to all of us,

02:03:01.900 --> 02:03:03.667
and all of us must solve them.

02:03:03.667 --> 02:03:04.661
Thank you very much.

02:03:04.661 --> 02:03:07.643
[APPLAUSE]

02:03:33.040 --> 02:03:36.091
PEARCE: Please join me again
in thanking Mr. Ted Childs.

02:03:36.091 --> 02:03:38.953
[APPLAUSE]

02:03:44.680 --> 02:03:47.168
Certainly your words were
quite timely in helping

02:03:47.168 --> 02:03:49.210
us to recognize the seminal
moment that we've now

02:03:49.210 --> 02:03:52.640
found ourselves in.

02:03:52.640 --> 02:03:56.090
Now at this time, I would
like to welcome Provost Rafael

02:03:56.090 --> 02:04:00.530
Reif who will recognize the
2006, 2007 Martin Luther

02:04:00.530 --> 02:04:02.950
King, Jr. visiting
professors and scholars.

02:04:02.950 --> 02:04:06.260
The MLK visiting professors
and scholars programs

02:04:06.260 --> 02:04:08.584
are administered through
the provost's office.

02:04:08.584 --> 02:04:10.066
Professor Reif.

02:04:10.066 --> 02:04:13.030
[APPLAUSE]

02:04:20.450 --> 02:04:23.880
REIF: Mr. Childs, thank you
indeed for a very inspiring

02:04:23.880 --> 02:04:24.380
speech.

02:04:24.380 --> 02:04:29.210
And Tabitha, I would
like to say here publicly

02:04:29.210 --> 02:04:31.930
that I identify strongly with
your background and experience,

02:04:31.930 --> 02:04:33.430
and I found your
speech very moving.

02:04:36.720 --> 02:04:42.290
I am honored here today to
recognize our MLK visiting

02:04:42.290 --> 02:04:45.000
professors and scholars.

02:04:45.000 --> 02:04:48.620
This program was established
to recognize the contributions

02:04:48.620 --> 02:04:51.300
of outstanding scholars.

02:04:51.300 --> 02:04:54.620
Since the first
appointments in 1995,

02:04:54.620 --> 02:04:59.300
we have had close to 40
visiting scholars among us.

02:04:59.300 --> 02:05:02.220
This year, as President
Hockfield said earlier,

02:05:02.220 --> 02:05:05.900
we have eight outstanding
MLK visiting scholars,

02:05:05.900 --> 02:05:09.050
enriching our MIT community
with their presence.

02:05:09.050 --> 02:05:10.910
And I would like to
introduce them and say

02:05:10.910 --> 02:05:14.010
a few words about each of them.

02:05:14.010 --> 02:05:16.730
The first one is Professor
William M. Harris,

02:05:16.730 --> 02:05:19.730
Sr. Department of Urban
Studies and Planning.

02:05:19.730 --> 02:05:21.970
He was here this morning
actually sharing the table

02:05:21.970 --> 02:05:22.470
with me.

02:05:22.470 --> 02:05:25.130
He had to leave a
few minutes ago.

02:05:25.130 --> 02:05:27.920
He's a professor of Urban
and Regional Planning

02:05:27.920 --> 02:05:30.590
at Jackson State University.

02:05:30.590 --> 02:05:33.600
He holds memberships in
several organizations,

02:05:33.600 --> 02:05:36.470
including the Urban Affairs
Association and the American

02:05:36.470 --> 02:05:38.900
Planning Association.

02:05:38.900 --> 02:05:41.210
Professor Harris has
received numerous awards

02:05:41.210 --> 02:05:44.510
and recognitions,
including more than 1,000

02:05:44.510 --> 02:05:47.660
outstanding community
service awards.

02:05:47.660 --> 02:05:49.490
And most of his
sponsored research

02:05:49.490 --> 02:05:53.210
has been directly related to
African-American low income

02:05:53.210 --> 02:05:55.640
populations.

02:05:55.640 --> 02:05:59.419
Next, I would like to introduce
Professor Dale Joachim.

02:05:59.419 --> 02:06:00.752
Dale, would you please stand up?

02:06:00.752 --> 02:06:03.716
[APPLAUSE]

02:06:07.180 --> 02:06:10.420
Before breakfast, I asked
Dale to help me practice

02:06:10.420 --> 02:06:13.117
pronounce his last name.

02:06:13.117 --> 02:06:14.950
I know I'm doing a
terrible job at it, Dale,

02:06:14.950 --> 02:06:18.070
but I'm doing the best I can.

02:06:18.070 --> 02:06:21.340
Dale's here in the Media,
Arts, and Sciences Program.

02:06:21.340 --> 02:06:24.520
He's a member of the faculty
of Tulane University, where

02:06:24.520 --> 02:06:28.510
he instituted this Speech and
Sound Laboratory for research

02:06:28.510 --> 02:06:31.540
on audio signature
detection, identification,

02:06:31.540 --> 02:06:33.340
and localization.

02:06:33.340 --> 02:06:36.190
He has taught computer
architecture, digital logic,

02:06:36.190 --> 02:06:39.040
and speech processing
courses at Tulane.

02:06:39.040 --> 02:06:42.790
Before joining Tulane, he worked
for Sanders Lockheed Martin

02:06:42.790 --> 02:06:46.250
in senior data systems.

02:06:46.250 --> 02:06:49.000
The next one is Professor
Ainissa Ramirez.

02:06:49.000 --> 02:06:50.530
I didn't see her this morning.

02:06:50.530 --> 02:06:53.200
Is she here with us?

02:06:53.200 --> 02:06:56.770
Ainissa was here until recently
in the Department of Material

02:06:56.770 --> 02:06:58.420
Science and Engineering.

02:06:58.420 --> 02:07:02.860
She is a professor of mechanical
engineering at Yale University.

02:07:02.860 --> 02:07:06.460
In 2003, Technology
Review named her

02:07:06.460 --> 02:07:10.580
as one of the world's
top 100 young innovators,

02:07:10.580 --> 02:07:14.410
what we refer to as
TR100, for her discovery

02:07:14.410 --> 02:07:18.790
of a universal solder that can
bond metals to ceramics class

02:07:18.790 --> 02:07:21.400
diamonds and oxide materials.

02:07:21.400 --> 02:07:24.280
She is a strong advocate
of improving the public's

02:07:24.280 --> 02:07:26.180
understanding of science.

02:07:26.180 --> 02:07:29.170
She lectures widely, and
she has been also a science

02:07:29.170 --> 02:07:32.380
correspondent for Time Magazine.

02:07:32.380 --> 02:07:35.650
Next I would like to introduce
Professor Akalu Tefera.

02:07:35.650 --> 02:07:37.123
Please stand up.

02:07:37.123 --> 02:07:40.069
[APPLAUSE]

02:07:43.520 --> 02:07:46.750
Professor Tefera is with
us in the Math department.

02:07:46.750 --> 02:07:49.620
He is a professor of math
at Grand Valley State

02:07:49.620 --> 02:07:51.690
University in Michigan.

02:07:51.690 --> 02:07:54.420
He enjoys teaching
math at many levels

02:07:54.420 --> 02:07:57.750
and has taught courses in
calculus, linear algebra,

02:07:57.750 --> 02:08:00.180
discrete math,
differential equations,

02:08:00.180 --> 02:08:02.010
and advanced calculus.

02:08:02.010 --> 02:08:04.560
In addition to undergraduate
teaching and curriculum

02:08:04.560 --> 02:08:06.330
development,
Professor Tefera has

02:08:06.330 --> 02:08:11.160
mentored undergraduate students
in cutting-edge research.

02:08:11.160 --> 02:08:15.675
Next, I would like to introduce
Dr. Dwight L. Williams.

02:08:15.675 --> 02:08:19.140
[APPLAUSE]

02:08:21.120 --> 02:08:23.460
Dwight is in the Department
of Nuclear Science

02:08:23.460 --> 02:08:24.690
and Engineering.

02:08:24.690 --> 02:08:29.220
He is presently the
principal nuclear physicist

02:08:29.220 --> 02:08:31.350
of the Science and
Technology Brain Trust

02:08:31.350 --> 02:08:34.140
in the US Department
of Defense, where

02:08:34.140 --> 02:08:39.130
he serves as the principal
advisor on all nuclear matters.

02:08:39.130 --> 02:08:42.180
He has numerous distinctions,
including a National Young

02:08:42.180 --> 02:08:45.300
Engineer of the Year Award,
and most recently, he

02:08:45.300 --> 02:08:47.820
was named a director of
National Intelligence

02:08:47.820 --> 02:08:51.090
Fellow, the highest
intelligence community award

02:08:51.090 --> 02:08:53.130
available to scientists.

02:08:53.130 --> 02:08:56.280
Dr. Williams is the first
African-American ever

02:08:56.280 --> 02:08:59.100
to win this award.

02:08:59.100 --> 02:09:02.370
Next I would like to
introduce Frank Espinosa.

02:09:02.370 --> 02:09:04.330
His name is really
Francisco Espinosa,

02:09:04.330 --> 02:09:06.150
but we Americanize it here.

02:09:06.150 --> 02:09:08.260
I think I didn't see
him this morning.

02:09:08.260 --> 02:09:10.360
Is Frank here?

02:09:10.360 --> 02:09:14.280
He's with the Media, Arts, and
Science, and Writing Program.

02:09:14.280 --> 02:09:17.310
He's presently the creator,
designer, and writer

02:09:17.310 --> 02:09:22.320
of Rocketo, A comic book series
published by Image Comics.

02:09:22.320 --> 02:09:24.450
Rocketo follows the
life and adventures

02:09:24.450 --> 02:09:29.310
of Rocketo Garrison, a world
famous explorer and mapmaker.

02:09:29.310 --> 02:09:33.570
Frank has worked extensively
for Disney and Warner Brothers.

02:09:33.570 --> 02:09:37.440
In 1992, he redesigned
the complete Looney Tunes

02:09:37.440 --> 02:09:38.670
characters.

02:09:38.670 --> 02:09:42.810
He has fashioned a series of
Looney Tunes US postage stamps

02:09:42.810 --> 02:09:45.330
and has produced an
award-winning character design

02:09:45.330 --> 02:09:49.630
manual for graphic
problem-solving strategies.

02:09:49.630 --> 02:09:52.560
Next, I would like to
introduce Eugene Gus Newport.

02:09:52.560 --> 02:09:54.468
Mr. Newport?

02:09:54.468 --> 02:09:57.330
[APPLAUSE]

02:10:00.670 --> 02:10:04.130
Gus is with us at the
Urban Studies and Planning

02:10:04.130 --> 02:10:05.320
Department.

02:10:05.320 --> 02:10:07.970
He is presently a senior
associate of the Urban

02:10:07.970 --> 02:10:11.510
Strategies Council, program
director of the Vanguard Public

02:10:11.510 --> 02:10:15.470
Foundation, and consultant to
the Louisiana Disaster Recovery

02:10:15.470 --> 02:10:16.730
Foundation.

02:10:16.730 --> 02:10:20.070
He has worked in several
capacities for federal, state,

02:10:20.070 --> 02:10:22.100
county, and
municipal governance,

02:10:22.100 --> 02:10:24.990
nonprofit agencies in
the private sector.

02:10:24.990 --> 02:10:27.590
I knew his name back
in the late '70s.

02:10:27.590 --> 02:10:31.030
At the time, I was a
grad student at Stanford.

02:10:31.030 --> 02:10:34.700
And Mr. Newport served as the
mayor of the city of Berkeley

02:10:34.700 --> 02:10:37.790
during that period, '79 to '86.

02:10:37.790 --> 02:10:41.330
He also served as vice president
from the US of the World Peace

02:10:41.330 --> 02:10:45.440
Council from '82 to 1990.

02:10:45.440 --> 02:10:48.230
Finally, I would like to
introduce Wilton Virgo, who

02:10:48.230 --> 02:10:50.150
I didn't see him this morning.

02:10:50.150 --> 02:10:52.430
Is Wilton here?

02:10:52.430 --> 02:10:54.320
He's in our
chemistry department.

02:10:54.320 --> 02:10:58.220
Dr. Virgo is presently a postdoc
working in Professor Robert

02:10:58.220 --> 02:11:00.150
Fields' laboratory.

02:11:00.150 --> 02:11:03.620
He received his PhD at Arizona
State and his undergrad degree

02:11:03.620 --> 02:11:05.540
in chemistry at Princeton.

02:11:05.540 --> 02:11:07.420
He has worked at
Brookhaven National Lab

02:11:07.420 --> 02:11:10.760
and has been the occasional
consultant for the American

02:11:10.760 --> 02:11:12.200
Tutoring Service.

02:11:12.200 --> 02:11:15.020
His research is in higher
resolution spectroscopy

02:11:15.020 --> 02:11:18.290
of transition metal
containing molecules.

02:11:18.290 --> 02:11:21.230
I would like to thank all
our visiting professors

02:11:21.230 --> 02:11:23.336
and scholars for
sharing their knowledge

02:11:23.336 --> 02:11:24.503
and enriching our community.

02:11:24.503 --> 02:11:25.952
Thank you very much.

02:11:25.952 --> 02:11:28.367
[APPLAUSE]

02:11:38.530 --> 02:11:40.717
PEARCE: Thank you very
much, Provost Reif.

02:11:40.717 --> 02:11:42.550
We will now continue
with some announcements

02:11:42.550 --> 02:11:45.340
relevant to our celebration.

02:11:45.340 --> 02:11:47.170
Those of you on
campus last week may

02:11:47.170 --> 02:11:50.380
have had the pleasure of seeing
the beautiful and informative

02:11:50.380 --> 02:11:53.090
installation
located in Lobby 10,

02:11:53.090 --> 02:11:56.650
which was designed and
constructed by the Dr. Martin

02:11:56.650 --> 02:11:59.830
Luther King, Jr. IEP seminar.

02:11:59.830 --> 02:12:01.480
The theme of the
installation was

02:12:01.480 --> 02:12:03.580
have we lived up to the dream?

02:12:03.580 --> 02:12:05.470
That's a really good question.

02:12:05.470 --> 02:12:07.090
Hopefully, each
of us can respond

02:12:07.090 --> 02:12:11.920
by knowing in our hearts that
we are striving to do just that.

02:12:11.920 --> 02:12:13.960
The installation,
which is described

02:12:13.960 --> 02:12:15.970
in the insert in your
program this morning,

02:12:15.970 --> 02:12:19.510
included a walkway that
visualized Dr. King's dream

02:12:19.510 --> 02:12:22.730
and what still needs to be done.

02:12:22.730 --> 02:12:25.960
In addition to the installation,
the students in the class

02:12:25.960 --> 02:12:28.390
are working on
several other projects

02:12:28.390 --> 02:12:31.570
involving children in the
community, a voter registration

02:12:31.570 --> 02:12:36.670
drive, T-shirts, and a work
on both the MIT@Lawrence

02:12:36.670 --> 02:12:39.550
Project and a
project with CASPAR,

02:12:39.550 --> 02:12:42.880
which stands for Cambridge
and Somerville Program

02:12:42.880 --> 02:12:47.050
for Alcoholism and Drug
Abuse Rehabilitation,

02:12:47.050 --> 02:12:49.660
both continuing into
the spring semester.

02:12:49.660 --> 02:12:52.180
Again, your insert
includes a description

02:12:52.180 --> 02:12:54.220
of all the projects.

02:12:54.220 --> 02:12:56.890
In addition, this morning you
may have picked up a flyer

02:12:56.890 --> 02:13:01.580
announcing an event,
Architecture Race Academe,

02:13:01.580 --> 02:13:05.690
the Black Architects Journey
Conference, which will be held

02:13:05.690 --> 02:13:09.410
March 16 and 17 here at MIT.

02:13:09.410 --> 02:13:11.720
Sponsored by the
Department of Architecture,

02:13:11.720 --> 02:13:15.800
this program is free
and open to the public.

02:13:15.800 --> 02:13:18.290
Now please welcome back Mr.
[? Hiram ?] [? Etienne ?]

02:13:18.290 --> 02:13:20.750
to lead us in singing the
black national anthem,

02:13:20.750 --> 02:13:24.500
"Lift Every Voice and Sing"
by James Weldon Johnson.

02:13:24.500 --> 02:13:28.190
We'd ask that you please
stand and join us in the song.

02:13:28.190 --> 02:13:31.454
The lyrics are located on
the back of your program.

02:13:31.454 --> 02:13:34.891
[MUSIC - "LIFT EVERY VOICE AND
 SING"]

02:13:47.690 --> 02:13:48.992
Help me out.

02:13:48.992 --> 02:13:56.970
(SINGING) Lift every voice
and sing till earth and heaven

02:13:56.970 --> 02:13:57.990
ring.

02:13:57.990 --> 02:14:03.710
Ring with the
harmonies of liberty.

02:14:06.420 --> 02:14:14.960
Let our rejoicing rise high
as the list'ning skies,

02:14:14.960 --> 02:14:20.736
let it resound loud
as the rolling sea.

02:14:24.510 --> 02:14:30.350
Sing a song full of the
faith that the dark past

02:14:30.350 --> 02:14:32.850
has taught us.

02:14:32.850 --> 02:14:38.984
Sing a song full of the
hope that the present

02:14:38.984 --> 02:14:43.800
has brought us.

02:14:43.800 --> 02:14:53.040
Facing the rising sun
of our new day begun,

02:14:53.040 --> 02:14:58.180
let us march on
till victory is won.

02:15:01.410 --> 02:15:10.150
Stony the road we trod,
bitter the chast'ning rod,

02:15:10.150 --> 02:15:18.590
felt in the days when
hope unborn had died.

02:15:18.590 --> 02:15:27.170
Yet with a steady beat,
have not our weary feet

02:15:27.170 --> 02:15:35.150
come to the place for
which our fathers sighed?

02:15:35.150 --> 02:15:45.045
Oh, we have come over a way that
with tears has been watered.

02:15:45.045 --> 02:15:50.600
We have come, treading
our path through the blood

02:15:50.600 --> 02:16:00.830
of the slaughtered, out of
the gloomy past, till now we

02:16:00.830 --> 02:16:08.620
stand at last where the white
gleam of our bright star

02:16:08.620 --> 02:16:09.457
is cast.

02:16:12.200 --> 02:16:20.830
God of our weary years,
God of our silent tears,

02:16:20.830 --> 02:16:29.240
thou who has brought
us thus far on the way,

02:16:29.240 --> 02:16:37.709
thou who has by thy might
led us into the light

02:16:37.709 --> 02:16:42.139
keep us forever in
the path, we pray.

02:16:47.040 --> 02:16:52.700
Lest our feet stray from
the places, our God,

02:16:52.700 --> 02:16:58.940
where we met thee,
lest our hearts, drunk

02:16:58.940 --> 02:17:06.809
with the wine of the
world, we forget thee,

02:17:06.809 --> 02:17:14.719
shadowed beneath thy
hand, may we forever

02:17:14.719 --> 02:17:23.019
stand true to our God,
true to our native land.

02:17:28.983 --> 02:17:32.459
[APPLAUSE]

02:17:32.459 --> 02:17:32.959
Thank you.

02:17:47.135 --> 02:17:49.510
PEARCE: Thank you so much for
that heartwarming rendition

02:17:49.510 --> 02:17:51.879
of "Lift Every Voice and Sing."

02:17:51.879 --> 02:17:54.879
Now I would like to ask Reverend
John [? Wusnick ?] of the MIT

02:17:54.879 --> 02:17:56.833
board of chaplains
to come forward

02:17:56.833 --> 02:17:59.250
to offer the benediction and
close this morning's program.

02:18:05.094 --> 02:18:05.594
Let us pray.

02:18:10.227 --> 02:18:12.352
Bless to us, oh God, the
song lifted from our lips.

02:18:17.320 --> 02:18:21.345
Relieve from us, oh God,
the malady of our racism.

02:18:24.080 --> 02:18:29.020
And bless to us, oh God,
the challenge of diversity

02:18:29.020 --> 02:18:34.459
and the challenge of
excellence, the possibilities

02:18:34.459 --> 02:18:37.920
of congruence.

02:18:37.920 --> 02:18:42.075
And lift from us, oh
God, injustice anywhere.

02:18:45.549 --> 02:18:51.469
Bind us, oh God, to the vision
we have for our friends,

02:18:51.469 --> 02:18:56.379
to the vision we have
for our colleagues.

02:18:56.379 --> 02:18:59.850
Bind us, oh God,
to the dreams we

02:18:59.850 --> 02:19:03.174
wish for our children and
our children's children.

02:19:06.090 --> 02:19:06.923
Amen.

02:19:06.923 --> 02:19:07.548
AUDIENCE: Amen.

02:19:17.290 --> 02:19:20.389
PEARCE: I certainly hope you've
enjoyed this morning's program.

02:19:20.389 --> 02:19:22.260
It is my sincere
desire that you've

02:19:22.260 --> 02:19:24.719
been inspired to not
only remember the words

02:19:24.719 --> 02:19:28.530
and work of Dr. King and his
wife, Coretta Scott King,

02:19:28.530 --> 02:19:31.070
but also to act upon them.

02:19:31.070 --> 02:19:33.500
Please remember also to drop
by the Black Students Union

02:19:33.500 --> 02:19:37.490
Lounge, which is right outside
these doors, after the program.

02:19:37.490 --> 02:19:40.580
Thank you once again for
joining us this morning,

02:19:40.580 --> 02:19:42.080
and we would be
delighted to see you

02:19:42.080 --> 02:19:45.020
next year for the program
and the celebration

02:19:45.020 --> 02:19:49.340
that we will have
on February 8, 2008.

02:19:49.340 --> 02:19:53.140
I leave you with these words
by William George Jordan.

02:19:53.140 --> 02:19:55.300
Into the hands of
every individual

02:19:55.300 --> 02:19:59.180
is given a marvelous
power for good or evil--

02:19:59.180 --> 02:20:04.260
the silent unconscious
unseen influence of his life.

02:20:04.260 --> 02:20:09.060
I urge you today to resolve
to use your power for good.

02:20:09.060 --> 02:20:11.460
Have an outstanding
and blessed day.

02:20:11.460 --> 02:20:14.810
[APPLAUSE]

